---
title: Focus and Interaction
description: Configure pointer focus, grouped focus, crosshairs, controlled cursors, keyboard navigation, built-in tooltips, selection, and spatial indexes.
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The DOM hosts, framework adapters, and React Native "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Chart"},{"type":"text","value":" provide point-level\ninteraction from each mark's emitted "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartPoint"},{"type":"text","value":" values and rendered scene\nprimitives. The defaults cover geometry-aware pointer or responder focus,\nlinear keyboard or accessibility navigation, activation, and an optional\nbuilt-in tooltip. Definitions own these policies; adapters mount them and report\nevents."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"default-behavior","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default behavior"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With no custom focus strategy:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"pointer movement finds the nearest point within "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxFocusDistance"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"pointer leave or cancellation clears unpinned focus"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the SVG uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tabIndex"},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0"},{"type":"text","value":" by default) when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"keyboard"},{"type":"text","value":" is enabled"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"focusing the SVG selects the first point in the keyboard task order"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"arrow keys move through points sorted by pixel x, then pixel y"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Home"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"End"},{"type":"text","value":" move to the first and last point"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Enter"},{"type":"text","value":" and Space toggle an enabled sticky tooltip and call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onSelect"},{"type":"text","value":" for\nthe focused point"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"pressing the pointer outside both the chart and the tooltip dismisses a pinned tooltip"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a configured selection controller receives the same focused point before\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onSelect"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a click focuses and selects the nearest point, or selects "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" on the\nblank surface"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the renderer's focus ring follows the primary point"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxFocusDistance"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"48"},{"type":"text","value":" scene pixels. Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tabIndex"},{"type":"text","value":" to control\nnormal tab-order participation while keeping keyboard handling enabled. Set\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"keyboard: false"},{"type":"text","value":" to remove keyboard navigation and force tab index "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"-1"},{"type":"text","value":".\nAuthored "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"whenFocused"},{"type":"text","value":" marks compose with the primary-point ring. Set\ndefinition "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focusRing: false"},{"type":"text","value":" only when authored focus geometry replaces that\nindicator."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"built-in-focus-ring","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Built-in focus ring"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass an options object to change the built-in ring without targeting generated\nSVG:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const definition = defineChart({\n  marks,\n  scales,\n  focusRing: {\n    radius: 4,\n    strokeWidth: 2,\n    fill: '#ffffff',\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radius"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"5"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strokeWidth"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"2.5"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fill"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"var(--ts-chart-focus-fill, Canvas)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stroke"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The focused point's resolved series color"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The object form still enables the ring. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" keeps every default and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":"\nremoves only the built-in ring. Set the same value on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"theme.focusRing"},{"type":"text","value":" to\nprovide a theme default. An explicitly supplied definition-level "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focusRing"},{"type":"text","value":"\nvalue takes precedence, including "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":". Composed views and facet\ncells share the outer chart's focus layer, so configure their focus ring on\nthe outer definition or theme. Their child specs reject both "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focusRing"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"theme.focusRing"},{"type":"text","value":". The definition compiles this presentation into ordinary\nscene dots, so SVG, Canvas, React Native, facets, polar marks, custom mark\nrenderers, and the optional motion renderer share the same values. The ring\nremains hidden from the accessibility tree; the chart root continues to report\nfocus and values. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radius"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strokeWidth"},{"type":"text","value":" accept finite nonnegative numbers,\nincluding zero. Invalid values use their defaults."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focusGuideX"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focusGuideY"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/focus/guide"},{"type":"text","value":" for\ndatum-bound rules, markers, and axis labels. Their active geometry uses stable\nstructural keys, so the optional motion renderer can animate rapid focus\nretargets without an application SVG or frame loop. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./marks/focus-guide.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Focus guide marks"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pointer: false"},{"type":"text","value":" when the application owns the pointer gesture but still\nwants chart focus, focus marks, and tooltips. Automatic pointer move, leave,\nand click handling stops; keyboard navigation remains independent. Resolve and\npaint application-owned focus through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"host.interaction"},{"type":"text","value":" or the interaction\ncontroller reported by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onRender"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"interaction-geometry","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interaction geometry"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When neither "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focus"},{"type":"text","value":" nor "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"spatialIndex"},{"type":"text","value":" is supplied, pointer resolution has two\nstages:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The topmost interactive scene primitive containing the pointer wins. The\nresolver traverses the final scene in reverse paint order and applies nested\ntranslations and clips."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If no primitive contains the pointer, its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interaction.affinity"},{"type":"text","value":" ranks the\nfallback. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"},{"type":"text","value":" compare distance from that axis boundary first and use\ncomplete geometry distance to break ties; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"xy"},{"type":"text","value":" compares complete geometry\ndistance; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"geometry"},{"type":"text","value":" has no off-shape fallback."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxFocusDistance"},{"type":"text","value":" applies to that primary boundary distance, not necessarily\nto the point used as the tooltip and keyboard anchor. A semantic point that is\nnot attached to a scene primitive retains anchor-based two-dimensional\ndistance."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The primitive is the geometry source of truth: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rect"},{"type":"text","value":" includes its rounded\ncorners, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dot"},{"type":"text","value":" uses its radius, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"area"},{"type":"text","value":" uses its polygon, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"polyline"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rule"},{"type":"text","value":" use their stroked paths. Its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interaction"},{"type":"text","value":" attaches either one semantic\npoint or the ordered points represented by a continuous primitive. Groups and\nlabels cannot carry interaction metadata."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Built-in marks attach these natural defaults:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mark"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scene primitive"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fallback"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"barY"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rounded rectangle"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"barX"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rounded rectangle"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lineY"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stroked polyline"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"areaY"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Filled area"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"areaX"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Filled area"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rect"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dot"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hexagon"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rect, circle, polygon"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"xy"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bandX"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rounded rectangle"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bandY"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rounded rectangle"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Facet layout rewrites the primitive's attached point references while leaving\nthe primitive in local coordinates. The resolver therefore observes the same\npost-layout translations and clips as SVG and Canvas instead of maintaining a\nsecond geometry copy. Inline mark states similarly return a resolved scene to\nthe host; during a transition, pointer selection intentionally follows that\ndestination scene rather than interpolating a second hit-test scene."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With an active axis viewport, the host calls\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"viewportInteractionPoints(scene, presentationPoints)"},{"type":"text","value":" before pointer,\nkeyboard, custom-focus, or spatial-index resolution. Off-window anchors from\nclipped viewport content remain in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scene.points"},{"type":"text","value":" but are not focus candidates;\nfixed-ownership mark points remain eligible. Direct scene consumers can pass\nthat filtered list as the optional final argument to\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"findNearestPoint(scene, x, y, maxDistance, points)"},{"type":"text","value":" so primitive hit testing\nuses the same candidate set."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For curved "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"polyline"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"area"},{"type":"text","value":" nodes, the current resolver uses the\nprimitive's structured point geometry. Exact picking against an optional\nauthored SVG path string remains a separate refinement."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Built-in axis focus modes compose painted containment with axis snapping. When\nthe pointer is inside an interactive primitive, the topmost painted mark seeds\nthe primary point. Outside painted geometry, the configured axis mode uses its\nnormal nearest-axis fallback. A custom strategy replaces this host behavior."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"focus-modes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Focus modes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a preset for built-in focus behavior:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { tooltip } from '@tanstack/charts/tooltip'\n\nconst groupedDownloads = defineChart(definition, {\n  focus: 'group-x',\n  tooltip,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preset"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pointer resolution"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Group returned to callbacks and tooltip"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keyboard navigation"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nearest"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Nearest painted geometry or point in two dimensions"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Primary point only"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every point"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nearest-x"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Containing painted mark; otherwise nearest x, then nearest y"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Primary point only"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every point"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nearest-y"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Containing painted mark; otherwise nearest y, then nearest x"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Primary point only"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every point"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"group-x"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Containing painted mark; otherwise nearest x, then nearest y at that x"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One point per group sharing the semantic x value; primary point first"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One representative per semantic x value"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"group-y"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Containing painted mark; otherwise nearest y, then nearest x at that y"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One point per group sharing the semantic y value; primary point first"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One representative per semantic y value"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grouping compares semantic values, including dates by timestamp. Duplicate\npoints with the same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"group"},{"type":"text","value":" value are reduced to one member in grouped focus."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The equivalent "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focusGroupX"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focusGroupY"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focusNearestX"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focusNearestY"},{"type":"text","value":"\nstrategy objects remain available from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/focus"},{"type":"text","value":" for composition\nor direct strategy use. The exact exported objects receive the same host-level\ncontainment behavior as their presets. A strategy that wraps or copies one of\nthem is custom and owns its complete pointer resolution."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focusGroupAngle"},{"type":"text","value":" is available from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/polar"},{"type":"text","value":". It resolves the\nnearest radial ray, groups points with the same semantic angle value, and\norders keyboard tasks by angle. Use it for grouped radar, polar-line, and\nradial-dot tooltips. Painted "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialArc"},{"type":"text","value":" geometry already participates in\ndefault nearest focus."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"crosshair-guides","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Crosshair guides"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"crosshair"},{"type":"text","value":" is a data-less presentation mark. It follows the chart's resolved\nfocus state without adding scale values or pointer targets:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { crosshair, defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value', color: 'series' }),\n    crosshair({\n      x: { label: true },\n      y: false,\n      strokeDasharray: '4 4',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleUtc },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear },\n  },\n\n  focus: 'group-x',\n  maxFocusDistance: Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The x guide is vertical and the y guide is horizontal. Both default to\nenabled; labels and the intersection marker default to disabled. Shared rule\noptions apply to both axes, and an axis object can override them. A label uses\nthe matching resolved tick label when available, falls back to the semantic\nvalue, and accepts a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"format"},{"type":"text","value":" callback. Labels are clamped to the chart surface\nand rendered with a configurable halo. Rules and the optional marker are\nclipped to the plot. For difference intervals such as stacked bars and areas,\nthe label formats the plotted "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x2"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y2"},{"type":"text","value":" endpoint so it matches the guide;\ntooltip formatting still reports the interval difference."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"crosshair<TXValue, TYValue>(options = {})"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts these top-level options:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Meaning"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"crosshair-${markIndex}"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stable mark and guide identity"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vertical rule or categorical band"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Horizontal rule or categorical band"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"marker"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Marker at the resolved x/y intersection"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"motion"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No mark-specific override"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Optional guide spring or tween configuration"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The shared rule options apply to both axes. The same fields on an axis object\noverride the shared value for that axis:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rule option"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Meaning"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stroke"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chart foreground"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rule color"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strokeOpacity"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0.35"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rule stroke opacity"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strokeWidth"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Nonnegative rule width"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strokeDasharray"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SVG-compatible stroke dash pattern"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An x or y axis object adds two options:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Axis option"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Meaning"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"label"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" for defaults or a label options object"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"band"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" for defaults or a band options object"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On a categorical axis, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"band"},{"type":"text","value":" replaces that axis rule with a plot-spanning\ncursor band centered on the focused value:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"marks: [\n  crosshair({\n    x: {\n      band: {\n        inset: 0,\n        radius: 3,\n        fill: '#64748b',\n        fillOpacity: 0.16,\n      },\n      label: true,\n    },\n    y: false,\n  }),\n  barY(rows, {\n    x: 'period',\n    y: 'value',\n    color: 'series',\n    inset: 4,\n  }),\n  crosshair({\n    x: false,\n    y: { strokeDasharray: '4 4', label: true },\n  }),\n]","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The first guide is an underlay because it precedes the bars; the second is an\noverlay. With a bar inset of 4 and band inset of 0, the cursor extends exactly\n4 pixels beyond each bar edge. Its x label shows the focused period; the y rule\nlabel shows the focused stack endpoint. Use separate crosshair marks whenever\naxes need different placement."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Band option"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Meaning"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"inset"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pixels removed from both scale-band edges; negative values create outset"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radius"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Corner radius"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fill"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chart foreground"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fill color"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fillOpacity"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0.12"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fill opacity"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stroke"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Optional outline color"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strokeOpacity"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Optional outline opacity"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strokeWidth"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Optional nonnegative outline width"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"opacity"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Opacity applied to the complete band"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"band: true"},{"type":"text","value":" uses those defaults. Band geometry uses the resolved scale\nbandwidth, spans the plot in the other direction, and remains clipped to the\nplot. A continuous scale or any other axis with zero bandwidth emits no band.\nThe axis label can still be enabled because "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"band"},{"type":"text","value":" replaces only the rule."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Label options control formatting and paint outside the plot:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Label option"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"format"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Matching scale tick label, then semantic value"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"offset"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"8"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fill"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chart foreground"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fillOpacity"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stroke"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"var(--ts-chart-crosshair-label-halo, Canvas)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strokeOpacity"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strokeWidth"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"3"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"opacity"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fontSize"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"11"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fontWeight"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Marker options control the resolved x/y intersection marker:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Marker option"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radius"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"4"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fill"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"var(--ts-chart-crosshair-marker-fill, Canvas)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fillOpacity"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stroke"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Focused point color, then guide or theme color"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strokeOpacity"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strokeWidth"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"2"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"opacity"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass semantic axis types to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"crosshair"},{"type":"text","value":" when TypeScript should check label\nformatters independently from the surrounding definition:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"crosshair<Date, number>({\n  x: { label: { format: (value) => value.toISOString() } },\n  y: { label: { format: (value) => value.toFixed(1) } },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mark order controls placement. Put "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"crosshair(...)"},{"type":"text","value":" before the first ordinary\nmark for an underlay, or after it for an overlay. The default primary focus\nring still composes with the crosshair; set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focusRing: false"},{"type":"text","value":" only when the\ncrosshair marker or other authored geometry deliberately replaces it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The guide is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aria-hidden"},{"type":"text","value":". Pointer and keyboard users reach the same focus\nstate through the chart root, callbacks, and optional tooltip. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"motion"},{"type":"text","value":" on the\ncrosshair controls its keyed rules, bands, labels, and marker when the\ndefinition is mounted with the optional motion renderer. Active guide\nplacements remain visible through keyed scene updates so restored focus\nanimates from the previous geometry."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"controlled-cursors","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controlled cursors"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Import the controller and host extension from the isolated cursor entry when\ninteraction state must be shared or set programmatically:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createChartCursor, cursorHost } from '@tanstack/charts/cursor'\n\nconst sharedDate = createChartCursor<Date, number>()\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value' }),\n    crosshair({ x: { label: true }, y: false }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleUtc },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear },\n  },\n\n  focus: 'group-x',\n  cursor: {\n    use: cursorHost,\n    controller: sharedDate,\n    mode: 'focus',\n    match: 'x',\n    pin: true,\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"function createChartCursor<\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n>(\n  initialState: ChartCursorState<TXValue, TYValue> | null = null,\n): ChartCursorController<TXValue, TYValue>","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every definition cursor binding accepts these common fields:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Meaning"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"use"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Required"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cursor host extension, normally "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cursorHost"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"controller"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Required"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Observable structural cursor controller"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mode"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Required"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focus"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"free"},{"type":"text","value":" binding discriminator"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pin"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Enables activation to pin and dismiss"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mode-specific fields are:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mode"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Meaning"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focus"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"match"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"xy"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Semantic axes shared between hosts"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"free"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scale inverse"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Optional x-axis "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"valueAt"},{"type":"text","value":" semantic override"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"free"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scale inverse"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Optional y-axis "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"valueAt"},{"type":"text","value":" semantic override"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A free cursor inverts each coordinate through that destination scene's\nresolved scale by default. Reversed ranges and responsive plot geometry need\nno copied scale. A missing or non-invertible scale requires an explicit\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"valueAt(context)"},{"type":"text","value":" callback for that axis. Use the callback to replace default\ninversion with observed-value snapping, rounding, or another semantic mapping;\nreturn "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":" for an intentionally coordinate-only axis."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createChartCursor"},{"type":"text","value":" remains a three-method structural store. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cursorHost"},{"type":"text","value":" opts\nthe binding into platform-neutral cursor policy without adding that policy to\ncharts that do not use cursors. Adapter and renderer authors can import the\nprojection, focus, presentation, and session helpers from\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/cursor/host"},{"type":"text","value":"; application code normally uses the token from\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/cursor"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartCursorExtensionToken"},{"type":"text","value":" is the environment-neutral binding contract.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cursorHost"},{"type":"text","value":" implements it as a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartCursorHostExtension"},{"type":"text","value":". Adapter authors\ncall "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createChartCursorHostSession(binding)"},{"type":"text","value":" to create an ownership-safe\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartCursorHostSession"},{"type":"text","value":" for one mounted binding."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"That host entry uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createFocusChartCursorState"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createFreeChartCursorState"},{"type":"text","value":" to publish state, then\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveChartCursorPresentation"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveChartCursorFocus"},{"type":"text","value":" to project it\ninto each scene. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveChartPointerFocus"},{"type":"text","value":" composes built-in axis focus with\npainted containment and returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":" for default nearest focus so a host\ncan apply its spatial index or renderer geometry. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveChartFocusStrategy"},{"type":"text","value":"\nconverts a built-in focus name to its grouping strategy.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveFocusPresentation"},{"type":"text","value":" finally produces renderer-neutral underlay and\noverlay nodes. An empty array from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveChartPointerFocus"},{"type":"text","value":" means an explicit\nstrategy found no target. Pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scene.points"},{"type":"text","value":" (the default) as its final\nargument to enable painted containment. A distinct array explicitly signals\nactive renderer presentation points and preserves anchor resolution while\nthose interpolated points are authoritative."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the same controller in several browser or React Native definitions to\nsynchronize by semantic value rather than pixel position. In "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focus"},{"type":"text","value":" mode,\npointer, responder, keyboard, or accessibility focus publishes a\nvalue-anchored cursor. Each subscribing host maps the value through its own\nscales, resolves its own local point and focus group, and paints its crosshair\nand tooltip. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"match"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"xy"},{"type":"text","value":"; choose "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"},{"type":"text","value":" for an axis cursor.\nThe originating point's group is retained as the preferred series when more\nthan one local point matches. Optional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartCursorState.origin"},{"type":"text","value":" carries\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ key, markId, datumIndex }"},{"type":"text","value":" to retain the local point when equal values\nrepeat, including across facets. A unique "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":" plus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"markId"},{"type":"text","value":" survives data\nreordering; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"datumIndex"},{"type":"text","value":" disambiguates duplicate keys. A consuming scene\nignores the tie-breaker when its key and mark do not exist, then resolves from\nthe portable semantic "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"value"},{"type":"text","value":" and preferred "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"group"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"free"},{"type":"text","value":" mode follows plot coordinates without resolving a datum:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const freeCursor = createChartCursor<Date, number>()\n\nconst definition = defineChart(baseDefinition, {\n  cursor: {\n    use: cursorHost,\n    controller: freeCursor,\n    mode: 'free',\n    pin: true,\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The host publishes normalized plot coordinates so the free cursor survives a\nresponsive relayout, while resolved-scale inversion adds semantic values for\nlabels and application state. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"valueAt"},{"type":"text","value":" is an explicit replacement when the\ninteraction needs snapping or another precision policy. Programmatic state may\ninstead use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"anchor: 'value'"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"anchor: 'normalized'"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"anchor: 'scene'"},{"type":"text","value":". Only the anchor's coordinate\nspace is authoritative; the other fields are diagnostics from the host that\nlast emitted the state."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createChartCursor"},{"type":"text","value":" returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getState"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setState"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":". Passing\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" clears the cursor, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setState"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts a previous-state updater.\nUnpinned host-owned state clears on pointer or responder cancellation, leave,\nand blur. Cleanup is ownership-safe: a host clears only the exact unpinned\nstate object it most recently published to that controller. State replaced by\nthe application or another host, and pinned state, survives that host's\ncleanup, rebinding, and unmount."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pin: true"},{"type":"text","value":", click or tap pins. Browser focus cursors toggle through Enter\nor Space and dismiss through Escape; React Native focus cursors expose the\nequivalent activate and escape accessibility actions. A pinned free cursor can\nbe toggled by another click or tap, or cleared programmatically."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Free mode deliberately does not invent keyboard datum navigation. When its\nvalues are part of the reader's task, pair it with a labeled semantic control\nor status output. A focus-mode cursor inherits the chart's existing keyboard\nnavigation and accessible tooltip behavior."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Definition cursor bindings have browser and React Native host parity. DOM\nrenderer hosts publish pointer and keyboard interaction. React Native "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Chart"},{"type":"text","value":"\npublishes responder gestures and, for focus mode, accessibility navigation,\nactivation, and dismissal. Both hosts subscribe to programmatic updates and\nshared controllers. Free mode deliberately remains a coordinate gesture with\nno invented keyboard or accessibility datum order."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"controlled-keyed-selection","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controlled keyed selection"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Import controlled semantic selection from its exact subpath:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\nimport { keyedSelection, whenSelected } from '@tanstack/charts/selection'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"keyedSelection(options)"},{"type":"text","value":" returns a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"KeyedSelection"},{"type":"text","value":" controller. Its\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"KeyedSelectionOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" contain:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Meaning"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"selected"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controlled signal holding a semantic key or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":", with typed reasons"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Maps a datum and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ point }"},{"type":"text","value":" context to a semantic key or a nullish skip"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The key callback's second parameter is a typed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"KeyedSelectionKeyContext"},{"type":"text","value":".\nControlled interaction state uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ControlledSignal<TValue, TReason>"},{"type":"text","value":"; its\nchange callback receives the proposed value and a\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ControlledSignalChangeContext<TReason>"},{"type":"text","value":" containing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ reason }"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Place that controller on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartDefinitionOptions.selection"},{"type":"text","value":". Pointer click,\nEnter, and Space pass the activated point to the controller. A blank-surface\nclick clears an existing selection. The controlled signal proposes a complete\nreplacement; Charts does not mutate its snapshot. Rebuild the definition with\nthe accepted value."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"KeyedSelectionChange"},{"type":"text","value":" is a discriminated union:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ type: 'select', value, point, source }"},{"type":"text","value":" proposes a non-null semantic key;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ type: 'clear', value: null, point: null, source }"},{"type":"text","value":" proposes clearing it."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"source"},{"type":"text","value":" is the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartSelectionSource"},{"type":"text","value":" value "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pointer"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"keyboard"},{"type":"text","value":". Selecting\nthe currently selected key still emits "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":", so application policy can\ndecide whether repeated activation is a no-op or another action. Returning\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":" ignores activation for that point."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"whenSelected(mark, selection)"},{"type":"text","value":" returns the same ordinary mark filtered by the\ncontrolled key after domains resolve. The full mark still contributes scale\nchannels and domains. Its final geometry retains every fragment owned by a\nmatching point, while interaction metadata and points are removed. It is\ntherefore a decorative overlay and cannot create a duplicate focus or\nactivation target. Duplicate semantic keys intentionally paint every match."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Selection does not replace focus, tooltip pinning, or application UI. A linked\nHTML table, live status, clear action, and persistence remain outside the\ndefinition and can update the same controlled key."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"continuous-cursor","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Continuous cursor"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Import "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"continuousCursor"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/interaction/cursor"},{"type":"text","value":" and place\nit in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartDefinitionOptions.controls"},{"type":"text","value":". Its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"position"},{"type":"text","value":" is a controlled\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ContinuousCursorPosition<TXValue, TYValue> | null"},{"type":"text","value":". Both values must be\nnumbers or dates backed by invertible scales."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ContinuousCursorChange"},{"type":"text","value":" reports one of:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"preview"},{"type":"text","value":" for pointer or touch movement, leave, and cancellation;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commit"},{"type":"text","value":" when click or tap proposes a pinned position; or"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clear"},{"type":"text","value":" when a pinned cursor is toggled off or Escape is pressed."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every reason includes the proposed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"value"},{"type":"text","value":", the controlled "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"origin"},{"type":"text","value":", a\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"source"},{"type":"text","value":", and a more specific "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cause"},{"type":"text","value":". Rebuild the definition with an accepted\ncommit or clear. Pointer previews remain host-local while the controlled value\nis "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":", avoiding a chart-scene render for each movement."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"xRule"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"yRule"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"marker"},{"type":"text","value":" configure renderer-neutral guide geometry and\nare enabled by default. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"xLabel"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"yLabel"},{"type":"text","value":" are opt-in and accept semantic\nformatters plus side, offset, padding, background, text, stroke, and typography\noptions. The behavior resolves all geometry against the final plot and scale\nranges."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SVG and Canvas DOM hosts mount the same contained pointer overlay. Static SVG\nand React Native retain only the guide for an accepted non-null position. The\noverlay is presentation-only, so applications should pair it with semantic\nsliders or inputs, status text, and any persistence or rounding policy."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This differs from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focusGuideX"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focusGuideY"},{"type":"text","value":": focus guides retarget to a\ndatum-owned chart point and participate in mark motion, while\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"continuousCursor"},{"type":"text","value":" tracks an unsnapped value pair without creating a focus or\nselection point."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The public cursor types divide state, event, and presentation concerns:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Contract"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ContinuousCursorValue"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A finite "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"number"},{"type":"text","value":" or valid "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Date"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ContinuousCursorPosition<TX, TY>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The semantic x/y pair"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ContinuousCursorPointerSource"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pointer or touch preview/commit source"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ContinuousCursorSource"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pointer, touch, or keyboard clear source"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ContinuousCursorChange<TX, TY>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preview, commit, and clear reason union"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ContinuousCursorRuleOptions"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rule stroke, opacity, width, dash, and cap"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ContinuousCursorMarkerOptions"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Marker radius, fill, stroke, and opacity"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ContinuousCursorLabelOptions<TValue>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Formatter, side, spacing, box, text, stroke, and typography"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ContinuousCursorOptions<TXValue, TYValue>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controlled position plus x/y rule, marker, and label configuration"}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"horizontal-brush","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Horizontal brush"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Import "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"brushX"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/interaction/brush"},{"type":"text","value":" and place it in\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartDefinitionOptions.controls"},{"type":"text","value":". Its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"range"},{"type":"text","value":" is a controlled\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BrushRange<TValue>"},{"type":"text","value":" with inclusive semantic "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"start"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"end"},{"type":"text","value":" values."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BrushXChange"},{"type":"text","value":" reports "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"preview"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commit"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cancel"},{"type":"text","value":", the proposed and\norigin ranges, the pointer or keyboard source, and whether the selection,\nstart, end, or a new blank region was manipulated. Explicit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"values"},{"type":"text","value":" determine\norder, snapping, slider indices, and keyboard steps. String values require\nthem. Number and Date values may instead use scale inversion only when\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"keyboard: false"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BrushXValuesOptions<TValue>"},{"type":"text","value":" is the candidate-backed form.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"format"},{"type":"text","value":" supplies each keyboard handle's value text. Its\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BrushXFormatContext"},{"type":"text","value":" names the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"start"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"end"},{"type":"text","value":" handle, so values that mark\nboundaries between items, such as bar edges, can name the first item after the\nstart handle and the last item before the end handle.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BrushXContinuousOptions<number | Date>"},{"type":"text","value":" is the invertible, pointer-only form.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BrushXSource"},{"type":"text","value":" distinguishes pointer and keyboard commits, while\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BrushXTarget"},{"type":"text","value":" identifies the selection, start handle, end handle, or newly\ndrawn region."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The behavior resolves against the final x scale and plot bounds. SVG and\nCanvas DOM hosts mount the same D3-backed overlay and contain its events before\nnormal chart focus or selection. Static SVG and React Native paint the\nrenderer-neutral range and handles without interactive host controls. The\napplication still owns fixed-window expansion, validation, linked-view layout,\nstatus text, persistence, and any native semantic control."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"horizontal-scale-handle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Horizontal scale handle"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Import "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"handleX"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/interaction/handle"},{"type":"text","value":" and place it in\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartDefinitionOptions.controls"},{"type":"text","value":". It binds one controlled x value to an\nexplicit ordered candidate list. The controlled value must equal one of those\ncandidates."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HandleXOptions<TXValue, TYValue>"},{"type":"text","value":" contains:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Contract"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"value"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ControlledSignal<TXValue, HandleXChange<TXValue>>"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"values"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ordered candidates for scale positioning, snapping, and keyboard steps"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cross"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ edge: 'top' | 'bottom', offset? }"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ value: TYValue }"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"trackStyle"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scene style for the horizontal candidate track"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ruleStyle"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scene style for the vertical rule, or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"handleStyle"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scene style for the current-value handle"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hitSize"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pointer target height and end padding; defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"44"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ariaLabel"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Slider name; defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Horizontal value"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"format"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Formats the semantic value for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aria-valuetext"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"keyboard"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Enables the slider keyboard task; defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stable behavior and host-control identity"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An edge cross places the track outside the corresponding plot edge by its\noptional offset. A value cross maps the supplied semantic y value through the\nfinal y scale and places the track inside the plot. For an edge cross, the\nvertical rule spans from the opposite plot edge to the track. For a value\ncross, it spans from the plot top to that value."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HandleXCross<TValue>"},{"type":"text","value":" names this edge-or-value union.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HandleXPointerSource"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pointer | touch"},{"type":"text","value":"; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HandleXSource"},{"type":"text","value":" adds "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"keyboard"},{"type":"text","value":"\nfor committed and canceled changes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HandleXChange<TValue>"},{"type":"text","value":" is a discriminated union. Pointer and touch gestures\npropose "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"preview"},{"type":"text","value":", then "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commit"},{"type":"text","value":" on release or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cancel"},{"type":"text","value":" with the origin value.\nArrow keys move one candidate, Home and End move to the first and last\ncandidate, and every keyboard change commits immediately. Each reason carries\nthe proposed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"value"},{"type":"text","value":", gesture "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"origin"},{"type":"text","value":", and pointer, touch, or keyboard\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"source"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The behavior resolves against the final x and optional y scales. SVG and\nCanvas DOM hosts mount the same contained slider overlay and preserve an\nactive gesture across accepted controlled updates. Static SVG and React Native\npaint the accepted track, rule, and handle without an interactive control.\nApplications retain playback timing, status, persistence, and native semantic\ncontrols."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"horizontal-zoom","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Horizontal zoom"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Import "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"zoomX"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/interaction/zoom"},{"type":"text","value":" and place it in\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartDefinitionOptions.controls"},{"type":"text","value":". Its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"window"},{"type":"text","value":" is a controlled\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ZoomXWindow<TValue>"},{"type":"text","value":" with semantic "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"start"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"end"},{"type":"text","value":" values. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TValue"},{"type":"text","value":" must be\na number or Date backed by an invertible x scale."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"extent"},{"type":"text","value":" defines the complete allowed x domain. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleExtent"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults to\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[1, Infinity]"},{"type":"text","value":"; its first value must be "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1"},{"type":"text","value":", and its second value sets the\nmaximum zoom factor relative to the extent. The configured x scale must use\nthe controlled window as its domain; accepted proposals rebuild the definition\nwith that same window."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The behavior resolves against the final x scale and plot bounds. Its contained\nSVG or Canvas host control owns pointer-anchored vertical-wheel zoom, drag and\nhorizontal-wheel pan, pointer and touch input, delta-mode normalization,\nclamping, cancellation, and teardown. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"wheelActivation"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focus"},{"type":"text","value":", so\nwheel input is captured only while the plot control is focused. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"modifier"},{"type":"text","value":"\nto capture Control+wheel or Command+wheel under the pointer without prior focus\nwhile every unmodified wheel continues to scroll the page. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"always"},{"type":"text","value":" only\nwhen the chart should capture every wheel over its plot. Plus and minus zoom\naround the center, arrow keys pan, Home proposes the full extent, and Escape\ncancels an active gesture."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ZoomXChange"},{"type":"text","value":" reports "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"preview"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commit"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cancel"},{"type":"text","value":"; the proposed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"value"},{"type":"text","value":",\nthe gesture "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"origin"},{"type":"text","value":", a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"zoom"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pan"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reset"},{"type":"text","value":" action, and a wheel, pointer,\ntouch, or keyboard source. Wheel streams and direct manipulation preview before\ntheir terminal event. Keyboard changes commit immediately. A cancel proposes\nthe origin window."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"keyboard: false"},{"type":"text","value":" only when an application supplies equivalent semantic\ncontrols. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ariaLabel"},{"type":"text","value":" names the plot control, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ariaDescription"},{"type":"text","value":" can replace its\ngenerated instructions, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"format"},{"type":"text","value":" formats values in those instructions, and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onActiveChange"},{"type":"text","value":" observes focus without moving accepted state into Charts."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Charts does not mutate the controlled snapshot. Keep visible-row filtering or\nclipping, y-domain policy, status, reset and recovery controls, follow-latest\nbehavior, and persistence in the application. Static SVG and React Native\nrender the accepted configured window but provide no zoom host control."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The public zoom types are:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Contract"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ZoomXValue"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A finite "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"number"},{"type":"text","value":" or valid "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Date"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ZoomXWindow<TValue>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Semantic "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"start"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"end"},{"type":"text","value":" values"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ZoomXSource"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wheel, pointer, touch, or keyboard origin"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ZoomXAction"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Zoom, pan, or reset action"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ZoomXWheelActivation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Focus, modifier-gated, or unconditional wheel capture"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ZoomXChange<TValue>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preview, commit, and cancel reason union"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ZoomXOptions<TValue>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controlled window, extent, limits, wheel, accessibility, and key options"}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"disabling-chart-owned-focus","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disabling chart-owned focus"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focus: false"},{"type":"text","value":" to disable chart-owned pointer and keyboard point focus. The\nscene keeps its semantic points but omits the generated default focus layer,\nand the DOM host forces the chart surface out of the tab order. Explicit\nfocus-only marks remain available to custom renderers and programmatic paint."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { focusDisabled } from '@tanstack/charts/focus/disabled'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focusDisabled"},{"type":"text","value":" resolves, groups, and navigates to no points. Use it when an\napplication owns gestures, selection paint, accessibility, and task semantics\noutside the native resolver but still needs the rendered focus layer. Set\ndefinition "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"keyboard: false"},{"type":"text","value":" and omit its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tooltip"},{"type":"text","value":" as appropriate for that\napplication-owned interaction."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"custom-focus-strategies","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom focus strategies"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface ChartFocusStrategy<\n  TDatum = unknown,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n> {\n  resolve(\n    points: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[],\n    context: ChartFocusResolveContext,\n  ): readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[]\n\n  group(\n    points: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[],\n    context: ChartFocusGroupContext<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>,\n  ): readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[]\n\n  navigation(\n    points: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[],\n  ): readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[]\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartFocusResolveContext"},{"type":"text","value":" contains scene-pixel "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxDistance"},{"type":"text","value":".\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolve"},{"type":"text","value":" returns the primary point first. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartFocusGroupContext"},{"type":"text","value":" contains\nthe point restored or reached through keyboard navigation. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"navigation"},{"type":"text","value":"\nreturns the ordered keyboard task set."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartFocusMode"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartFocusPreset"},{"type":"text","value":" string or a\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartFocusStrategy"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"tooltips","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tooltips"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Import "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tooltip"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/tooltip"},{"type":"text","value":" and place it on the definition\nfor the native accessible tooltip. The default is a structured label-value\ntable. Grouped focus adds a shared-axis heading and one color swatch and value\nrow per series. Visible axis labels are reused. Numbers use browser locale\nformatting; dates use stable UTC ISO formatting."}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface ChartTooltipOptions<\n  TDatum = unknown,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n> {\n  className?: string\n  portal?: ChartTooltipPortalInput\n  items?: readonly ChartTooltipItem<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[]\n  sort?: ChartTooltipSort<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>\n  anchor?: ChartTooltipAnchor<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>\n  placement?: 'auto' | ChartTooltipPlacement | readonly ChartTooltipPlacement[]\n  offset?: number\n  content?: (\n    points: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[],\n    context: ChartTooltipContentContext,\n  ) => ChartTooltipContent\n  format?: (\n    point: ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>,\n    context: ChartTooltipContentContext,\n  ) => string\n  formatGroup?: (\n    points: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[],\n    context: ChartTooltipContentContext,\n  ) => string\n  sticky?: boolean\n  visibility?: 'focus' | 'pinned'\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Meaning"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"className"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Class appended after "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ts-chart-tooltip"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"portal"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Optional top-layer or fixed-position transport"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"items"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Automatic x/y"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ordered rows for a single focused point"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sort"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"visual"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grouped row order"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"anchor"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"point"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preset, per-axis coordinates, or coordinate resolver"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"placement"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"auto"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixed or ordered fallback box placements"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"offset"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"10"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scene-pixel gap between anchor and box"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"content"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Automatic rows"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Returns a safe title and structured rows"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"format"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Replaces content with primary-point text"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"formatGroup"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Replaces content with focused-group text"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sticky"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Enables activation-to-pin and text selection"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"visibility"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focus"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Shows on focus or only after activation with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pinned"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Formatting precedence is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"content"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"formatGroup"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"format"},{"type":"text","value":", then the default.\nThe text formatters do not parse HTML, and newlines are preserved. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"className"},{"type":"text","value":"\nis appended to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ts-chart-tooltip"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The default DOM surface inherits these optional CSS variables from the chart\ncontainer while retaining its built-in fallback values. A portaled Popover\nkeeps that ancestry. If the portal fallback moves the tooltip under\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ownerDocument.body"},{"type":"text","value":", chart-container-only variables no longer inherit; set\nthem on the tooltip class or a shared document ancestor instead:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Variable"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controls"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"--ts-chart-tooltip-background"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Surface background"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"--ts-chart-tooltip-color"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Text color"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"--ts-chart-tooltip-border"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete border declaration"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"--ts-chart-tooltip-border-radius"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Corner radius"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"--ts-chart-tooltip-shadow"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Box shadow"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"--ts-chart-tooltip-max-width"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Maximum width"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"--ts-chart-tooltip-padding"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inner spacing"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"--ts-chart-tooltip-font"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete font shorthand"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"className"},{"type":"text","value":" for content structure or additional selectors. Use the\nvariables for ordinary surface theming without specificity overrides."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartTooltipContentContext.pinned"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":" during transient inspection and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" after activation. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"content"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"format"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"formatGroup"},{"type":"text","value":", and item "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"text"},{"type":"text","value":"\nreceive the same context, so either structured or plaintext content can reveal\nadditional detail when pinned."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"ordered-point-items","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ordered point items"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"items"},{"type":"text","value":" is an ordered single-point row list. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"group"},{"type":"text","value":"\nshorthands, a configured channel, a scalar datum field, or derived text:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const detailedDefinition = defineChart(definition, {\n  tooltip: {\n    use: tooltip,\n    items: [\n      {\n        channel: 'y',\n        label: 'Revenue',\n        text: (point) => currency(point.yValue),\n      },\n      {\n        field: 'volume',\n        label: 'Volume',\n        text: (point) => compact(point.datum.volume),\n      },\n      {\n        id: 'change',\n        label: 'Change',\n        text: (point) =>\n          point.datum.change == null ? null : percent(point.datum.change),\n      },\n      'x',\n      'group',\n    ],\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Array order is row order. A nullish datum field or nullish "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"text"},{"type":"text","value":" result omits\nthe row. Adding "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"group"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"items"},{"type":"text","value":" renders it as a row instead of the automatic\nsingle-point title. In grouped focus, the shared-axis item supplies the heading\nlabel and text, the opposite-axis item formats values, and the group item\nformats series names. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sort"},{"type":"text","value":" orders those generated series rows. Additional\ngrouped structure belongs in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"content"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sort"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"visual"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color-domain"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focus"},{"type":"text","value":", or a typed point comparator.\nVisual order follows the marks across the screen: top-to-bottom for an x-group\nand left-to-right for a y-group."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"anchor-and-placement","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Anchor and placement"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"anchor"},{"type":"text","value":" controls the scene coordinate followed by the box:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"point"},{"type":"text","value":" follows the primary focused point."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pointer"},{"type":"text","value":" follows the current pointer and falls back to the point for\nkeyboard focus."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"group-center"},{"type":"text","value":" uses the center of the focused points' bounding box."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ x, y }"},{"type":"text","value":" chooses each coordinate from point, pointer, semantic value,\ngroup center, or a plot edge."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A resolver receives the focused points plus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ focus, pointer, plot, surface, scales }"},{"type":"text","value":" and returns scene coordinates. A nullish or non-finite\nresult falls back to the primary point."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"placement"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"auto"},{"type":"text","value":", one placement, or an ordered fallback list. The\nplacements are "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"top"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"top-right"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"right"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bottom-right"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bottom"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bottom-left"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"left"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"top-left"},{"type":"text","value":". A single placement is fixed and shifted\ninside the surface. A list uses the first placement that fits; if none fits,\nit uses the least-overflowing candidate and shifts it inside. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"auto"},{"type":"text","value":" uses\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"top"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bottom"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"right"},{"type":"text","value":", then "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"left"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const groupedDefinition = defineChart(definition, {\n  tooltip: {\n    use: tooltip,\n    anchor: { x: 'plot-center', y: 'plot-top' },\n    placement: ['top', 'right', 'left', 'bottom'],\n    offset: 12,\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Import "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"portal"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/tooltip/portal"},{"type":"text","value":" and assign it to the\ntooltip's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"portal"},{"type":"text","value":" option when an ancestor clips overflow or creates an\nincompatible stacking context. The host opens the tooltip as a manual Popover\nin the browser top layer where supported, while retaining its chart DOM\nancestry. If Popover is unavailable or fails, it moves the tooltip directly\nunder the chart's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ownerDocument"},{"type":"text","value":" body with fixed high-stack positioning. Both\npaths map the scene anchor to viewport coordinates, reposition during scroll,\nresize, and content resize, and collide against the viewport instead of the\nchart box."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Clicking, Enter, or Space pins the tooltip. The next activation unpins it.\nPressing the pointer outside the chart and tooltip, or pressing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Escape"},{"type":"text","value":",\nunpins and clears focus. Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sticky: false"},{"type":"text","value":" to disable pinning. A display-only\ntooltip has "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"role=\"status\""},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aria-live=\"polite\""},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"visibility: 'pinned'"},{"type":"text","value":" for click-or-keyboard detail that should not paint a\ntransient shell. Focus and inline mark states still update before activation;\nthe tooltip element and adapter body mount only while pinned."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"content"},{"type":"text","value":" supports display-only rows. Every framework adapter can compose\nnative content around those rows while preserving the definition's ordering,\nanchor, placement, portal, and pinning behavior. A pinned custom body has\nnon-modal dialog semantics."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"callbacks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Callbacks"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface ChartInteractionCallbacks {\n  onFocusChange?: (point: ChartPoint | null) => void\n  onFocusGroupChange?: (points: readonly ChartPoint[]) => void\n  onSelect?: (point: ChartPoint | null) => void\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Definitions infer callback datum and semantic x/y value types without adapter\ngenerics. Focus callbacks run only when the primary focus key changes, except\nthat a scene rebuild with an existing focused key reports the point with its\nnew coordinates and datum."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onSelect"},{"type":"text","value":" reports mouse clicks and keyboard activation. A click with no point\nreports "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":"; Enter and Space do nothing until a point is focused. When a\ndefinition has a selection controller, it receives the activation before the\nlegacy callback."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"spatial-indexes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Spatial indexes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The default lookup scans the cached scene targets linearly. Dense charts can\ninject an index:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"type ChartSpatialIndexFactory<\n  TDatum = unknown,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n> = (\n  points: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[],\n  context: ChartSpatialIndexFactoryContext<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>,\n) => ChartSpatialIndex<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The host rebuilds the index when the scene or definition changes. The index\nowns its search algorithm and must apply "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxDistance"},{"type":"text","value":". Point-only factories\ncan ignore the second argument; geometry-aware indexes can traverse\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context.scene"},{"type":"text","value":" and use primitive bounds as their acceleration layer.\nUse the granular spatial primitive appropriate to the data; the boundary is\ndescribed in "},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/scales-and-d3.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scales"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Supplying an index also replaces default primitive containment and affinity\nranking; the host does not add a linear safety scan after an indexed query. An\nindex that wants identical geometry semantics should index scene-primitive\nbounds and perform exact shape checks on its candidates."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A custom "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focus"},{"type":"text","value":" strategy takes precedence over "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"spatialIndex"},{"type":"text","value":" for pointer\nresolution."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"application-owned-gestures","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Application-owned gestures"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dragging, scrolling, custom crosshair overlays, and freeform range or lasso\nselections can listen on a wrapper or use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onRender"},{"type":"text","value":" to attach application\nbehavior to the default SVG or complete surface. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"handleX"},{"type":"text","value":" for one ordered\nscale value, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"brushX"},{"type":"text","value":" for a normal horizontal semantic range, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"zoomX"},{"type":"text","value":" for a\nnormal controlled x window. For custom gestures, keep semantic state outside\nthe scene and update a responsive definition by replacing its identity."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a definition cursor binding for snapped or free crosshairs. Keep other\nsemantic state outside the scene."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For application-timed datum inspection, use the stable controller instead of\nreimplementing coordinate conversion and focus:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const position = interaction.clientToScene(event.clientX, event.clientY)\nconst target = interaction.resolvePointer(event.clientX, event.clientY)\ninteraction.setControlledFocus(target)\ninteraction.setControlledFocus(null)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The resolution contains the scene position, primary point, and focus group.\nPassing that resolution to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setControlledFocus"},{"type":"text","value":" infers "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"source: 'pointer'"},{"type":"text","value":" and\nre-resolves its held position after scene and presentation updates. Passing a\nraw "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartPoint"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"source: 'programmatic'"},{"type":"text","value":"; either source can be\noverridden explicitly. Controlled focus is not cleared by unrelated\npointer-leave or focus-out events. Pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ pinned: true }"},{"type":"text","value":" to make an enabled\nsticky tooltip interactive."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Clean up application listeners before the next attachment or unmount.\nFor a completely independent renderer or interaction layer, use the scene and\nextension contracts in "},{"type":"link","href":"./custom-extensions.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom extensions"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"default-behavior","text":"Default behavior","level":2},{"id":"built-in-focus-ring","text":"Built-in focus ring","level":3},{"id":"interaction-geometry","text":"Interaction geometry","level":3},{"id":"focus-modes","text":"Focus modes","level":2},{"id":"crosshair-guides","text":"Crosshair guides","level":2},{"id":"controlled-cursors","text":"Controlled cursors","level":2},{"id":"controlled-keyed-selection","text":"Controlled keyed selection","level":2},{"id":"continuous-cursor","text":"Continuous cursor","level":2},{"id":"horizontal-brush","text":"Horizontal brush","level":2},{"id":"horizontal-scale-handle","text":"Horizontal scale handle","level":2},{"id":"horizontal-zoom","text":"Horizontal zoom","level":2},{"id":"disabling-chart-owned-focus","text":"Disabling chart-owned focus","level":2},{"id":"custom-focus-strategies","text":"Custom focus strategies","level":2},{"id":"tooltips","text":"Tooltips","level":2},{"id":"ordered-point-items","text":"Ordered point items","level":3},{"id":"anchor-and-placement","text":"Anchor and placement","level":3},{"id":"callbacks","text":"Callbacks","level":2},{"id":"spatial-indexes","text":"Spatial indexes","level":2},{"id":"application-owned-gestures","text":"Application-owned gestures","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nThe DOM hosts, framework adapters, and React Native `Chart` provide point-level\ninteraction from each mark's emitted `ChartPoint` values and rendered scene\nprimitives. The defaults cover geometry-aware pointer or responder focus,\nlinear keyboard or accessibility navigation, activation, and an optional\nbuilt-in tooltip. Definitions own these policies; adapters mount them and report\nevents.\n\n## Default behavior\n\nWith no custom focus strategy:\n\n- pointer movement finds the nearest point within `maxFocusDistance`\n- pointer leave or cancellation clears unpinned focus\n- the SVG uses `tabIndex` (`0` by default) when `keyboard` is enabled\n- focusing the SVG selects the first point in the keyboard task order\n- arrow keys move through points sorted by pixel x, then pixel y\n- `Home` and `End` move to the first and last point\n- `Enter` and Space toggle an enabled sticky tooltip and call `onSelect` for\n  the focused point\n- pressing the pointer outside both the chart and the tooltip dismisses a pinned tooltip\n- a configured selection controller receives the same focused point before\n  `onSelect`\n- a click focuses and selects the nearest point, or selects `null` on the\n  blank surface\n- the renderer's focus ring follows the primary point\n\n`maxFocusDistance` defaults to `48` scene pixels. Set `tabIndex` to control\nnormal tab-order participation while keeping keyboard handling enabled. Set\n`keyboard: false` to remove keyboard navigation and force tab index `-1`.\nAuthored `whenFocused` marks compose with the primary-point ring. Set\ndefinition `focusRing: false` only when authored focus geometry replaces that\nindicator.\n\n### Built-in focus ring\n\nPass an options object to change the built-in ring without targeting generated\nSVG:\n\n```ts\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks,\n  scales,\n  focusRing: {\n    radius: 4,\n    strokeWidth: 2,\n    fill: '#ffffff',\n  },\n})\n```\n\n| Option        | Default                                   |\n| ------------- | ----------------------------------------- |\n| `radius`      | `5`                                       |\n| `strokeWidth` | `2.5`                                     |\n| `fill`        | `var(--ts-chart-focus-fill, Canvas)`      |\n| `stroke`      | The focused point's resolved series color |\n\nThe object form still enables the ring. `true` keeps every default and `false`\nremoves only the built-in ring. Set the same value on `theme.focusRing` to\nprovide a theme default. An explicitly supplied definition-level `focusRing`\nvalue takes precedence, including `true` or `false`. Composed views and facet\ncells share the outer chart's focus layer, so configure their focus ring on\nthe outer definition or theme. Their child specs reject both `focusRing` and\n`theme.focusRing`. The definition compiles this presentation into ordinary\nscene dots, so SVG, Canvas, React Native, facets, polar marks, custom mark\nrenderers, and the optional motion renderer share the same values. The ring\nremains hidden from the accessibility tree; the chart root continues to report\nfocus and values. `radius` and `strokeWidth` accept finite nonnegative numbers,\nincluding zero. Invalid values use their defaults.\n\nUse `focusGuideX` or `focusGuideY` from `@tanstack/charts/focus/guide` for\ndatum-bound rules, markers, and axis labels. Their active geometry uses stable\nstructural keys, so the optional motion renderer can animate rapid focus\nretargets without an application SVG or frame loop. See\n[Focus guide marks](./marks/focus-guide.md).\n\nSet `pointer: false` when the application owns the pointer gesture but still\nwants chart focus, focus marks, and tooltips. Automatic pointer move, leave,\nand click handling stops; keyboard navigation remains independent. Resolve and\npaint application-owned focus through `host.interaction` or the interaction\ncontroller reported by `onRender`.\n\n### Interaction geometry\n\nWhen neither `focus` nor `spatialIndex` is supplied, pointer resolution has two\nstages:\n\n1. The topmost interactive scene primitive containing the pointer wins. The\n   resolver traverses the final scene in reverse paint order and applies nested\n   translations and clips.\n2. If no primitive contains the pointer, its `interaction.affinity` ranks the\n   fallback. `x` and `y` compare distance from that axis boundary first and use\n   complete geometry distance to break ties; `xy` compares complete geometry\n   distance; `geometry` has no off-shape fallback.\n\n`maxFocusDistance` applies to that primary boundary distance, not necessarily\nto the point used as the tooltip and keyboard anchor. A semantic point that is\nnot attached to a scene primitive retains anchor-based two-dimensional\ndistance.\n\nThe primitive is the geometry source of truth: `rect` includes its rounded\ncorners, `dot` uses its radius, `area` uses its polygon, and `polyline` and\n`rule` use their stroked paths. Its `interaction` attaches either one semantic\npoint or the ordered points represented by a continuous primitive. Groups and\nlabels cannot carry interaction metadata.\n\nBuilt-in marks attach these natural defaults:\n\n| Mark                     | Scene primitive       | Fallback |\n| ------------------------ | --------------------- | -------- |\n| `barY`                   | Rounded rectangle     | `x`      |\n| `barX`                   | Rounded rectangle     | `y`      |\n| `lineY`                  | Stroked polyline      | `x`      |\n| `areaY`                  | Filled area           | `x`      |\n| `areaX`                  | Filled area           | `y`      |\n| `rect`, `dot`, `hexagon` | Rect, circle, polygon | `xy`     |\n| `bandX`                  | Rounded rectangle     | `x`      |\n| `bandY`                  | Rounded rectangle     | `y`      |\n\nFacet layout rewrites the primitive's attached point references while leaving\nthe primitive in local coordinates. The resolver therefore observes the same\npost-layout translations and clips as SVG and Canvas instead of maintaining a\nsecond geometry copy. Inline mark states similarly return a resolved scene to\nthe host; during a transition, pointer selection intentionally follows that\ndestination scene rather than interpolating a second hit-test scene.\n\nWith an active axis viewport, the host calls\n`viewportInteractionPoints(scene, presentationPoints)` before pointer,\nkeyboard, custom-focus, or spatial-index resolution. Off-window anchors from\nclipped viewport content remain in `scene.points` but are not focus candidates;\nfixed-ownership mark points remain eligible. Direct scene consumers can pass\nthat filtered list as the optional final argument to\n`findNearestPoint(scene, x, y, maxDistance, points)` so primitive hit testing\nuses the same candidate set.\n\nFor curved `polyline` and `area` nodes, the current resolver uses the\nprimitive's structured point geometry. Exact picking against an optional\nauthored SVG path string remains a separate refinement.\n\nBuilt-in axis focus modes compose painted containment with axis snapping. When\nthe pointer is inside an interactive primitive, the topmost painted mark seeds\nthe primary point. Outside painted geometry, the configured axis mode uses its\nnormal nearest-axis fallback. A custom strategy replaces this host behavior.\n\n## Focus modes\n\nUse a preset for built-in focus behavior:\n\n```ts\nimport { tooltip } from '@tanstack/charts/tooltip'\n\nconst groupedDownloads = defineChart(definition, {\n  focus: 'group-x',\n  tooltip,\n})\n```\n\n| Preset      | Pointer resolution                                                     | Group returned to callbacks and tooltip                               | Keyboard navigation                     |\n| ----------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |\n| `nearest`   | Nearest painted geometry or point in two dimensions                    | Primary point only                                                    | Every point                             |\n| `nearest-x` | Containing painted mark; otherwise nearest x, then nearest y           | Primary point only                                                    | Every point                             |\n| `nearest-y` | Containing painted mark; otherwise nearest y, then nearest x           | Primary point only                                                    | Every point                             |\n| `group-x`   | Containing painted mark; otherwise nearest x, then nearest y at that x | One point per group sharing the semantic x value; primary point first | One representative per semantic x value |\n| `group-y`   | Containing painted mark; otherwise nearest y, then nearest x at that y | One point per group sharing the semantic y value; primary point first | One representative per semantic y value |\n\nGrouping compares semantic values, including dates by timestamp. Duplicate\npoints with the same `group` value are reduced to one member in grouped focus.\n\nThe equivalent `focusGroupX`, `focusGroupY`, `focusNearestX`, and `focusNearestY`\nstrategy objects remain available from `@tanstack/charts/focus` for composition\nor direct strategy use. The exact exported objects receive the same host-level\ncontainment behavior as their presets. A strategy that wraps or copies one of\nthem is custom and owns its complete pointer resolution.\n\n`focusGroupAngle` is available from `@tanstack/charts/polar`. It resolves the\nnearest radial ray, groups points with the same semantic angle value, and\norders keyboard tasks by angle. Use it for grouped radar, polar-line, and\nradial-dot tooltips. Painted `radialArc` geometry already participates in\ndefault nearest focus.\n\n## Crosshair guides\n\n`crosshair` is a data-less presentation mark. It follows the chart's resolved\nfocus state without adding scale values or pointer targets:\n\n```ts\nimport { crosshair, defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value', color: 'series' }),\n    crosshair({\n      x: { label: true },\n      y: false,\n      strokeDasharray: '4 4',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleUtc },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear },\n  },\n\n  focus: 'group-x',\n  maxFocusDistance: Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY,\n})\n```\n\nThe x guide is vertical and the y guide is horizontal. Both default to\nenabled; labels and the intersection marker default to disabled. Shared rule\noptions apply to both axes, and an axis object can override them. A label uses\nthe matching resolved tick label when available, falls back to the semantic\nvalue, and accepts a `format` callback. Labels are clamped to the chart surface\nand rendered with a configurable halo. Rules and the optional marker are\nclipped to the plot. For difference intervals such as stacked bars and areas,\nthe label formats the plotted `x2` or `y2` endpoint so it matches the guide;\ntooltip formatting still reports the interval difference.\n\n`crosshair<TXValue, TYValue>(options = {})` accepts these top-level options:\n\n| Option   | Default                   | Meaning                                      |\n| -------- | ------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |\n| `id`     | `crosshair-${markIndex}`  | Stable mark and guide identity               |\n| `x`      | `true`                    | Vertical rule or categorical band            |\n| `y`      | `true`                    | Horizontal rule or categorical band          |\n| `marker` | `false`                   | Marker at the resolved x/y intersection      |\n| `motion` | No mark-specific override | Optional guide spring or tween configuration |\n\nThe shared rule options apply to both axes. The same fields on an axis object\noverride the shared value for that axis:\n\n| Rule option       | Default          | Meaning                            |\n| ----------------- | ---------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| `stroke`          | Chart foreground | Rule color                         |\n| `strokeOpacity`   | `0.35`           | Rule stroke opacity                |\n| `strokeWidth`     | `1`              | Nonnegative rule width             |\n| `strokeDasharray` | None             | SVG-compatible stroke dash pattern |\n\nAn x or y axis object adds two options:\n\n| Axis option | Default | Meaning                                       |\n| ----------- | ------- | --------------------------------------------- |\n| `label`     | `false` | `true` for defaults or a label options object |\n| `band`      | `false` | `true` for defaults or a band options object  |\n\nOn a categorical axis, `band` replaces that axis rule with a plot-spanning\ncursor band centered on the focused value:\n\n```ts\nmarks: [\n  crosshair({\n    x: {\n      band: {\n        inset: 0,\n        radius: 3,\n        fill: '#64748b',\n        fillOpacity: 0.16,\n      },\n      label: true,\n    },\n    y: false,\n  }),\n  barY(rows, {\n    x: 'period',\n    y: 'value',\n    color: 'series',\n    inset: 4,\n  }),\n  crosshair({\n    x: false,\n    y: { strokeDasharray: '4 4', label: true },\n  }),\n]\n```\n\nThe first guide is an underlay because it precedes the bars; the second is an\noverlay. With a bar inset of 4 and band inset of 0, the cursor extends exactly\n4 pixels beyond each bar edge. Its x label shows the focused period; the y rule\nlabel shows the focused stack endpoint. Use separate crosshair marks whenever\naxes need different placement.\n\n| Band option     | Default          | Meaning                                                                  |\n| --------------- | ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `inset`         | `0`              | Pixels removed from both scale-band edges; negative values create outset |\n| `radius`        | None             | Corner radius                                                            |\n| `fill`          | Chart foreground | Fill color                                                               |\n| `fillOpacity`   | `0.12`           | Fill opacity                                                             |\n| `stroke`        | None             | Optional outline color                                                   |\n| `strokeOpacity` | None             | Optional outline opacity                                                 |\n| `strokeWidth`   | None             | Optional nonnegative outline width                                       |\n| `opacity`       | None             | Opacity applied to the complete band                                     |\n\n`band: true` uses those defaults. Band geometry uses the resolved scale\nbandwidth, spans the plot in the other direction, and remains clipped to the\nplot. A continuous scale or any other axis with zero bandwidth emits no band.\nThe axis label can still be enabled because `band` replaces only the rule.\n\nLabel options control formatting and paint outside the plot:\n\n| Label option    | Default                                        |\n| --------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |\n| `format`        | Matching scale tick label, then semantic value |\n| `offset`        | `8`                                            |\n| `fill`          | Chart foreground                               |\n| `fillOpacity`   | None                                           |\n| `stroke`        | `var(--ts-chart-crosshair-label-halo, Canvas)` |\n| `strokeOpacity` | None                                           |\n| `strokeWidth`   | `3`                                            |\n| `opacity`       | None                                           |\n| `fontSize`      | `11`                                           |\n| `fontWeight`    | None                                           |\n\nMarker options control the resolved x/y intersection marker:\n\n| Marker option   | Default                                         |\n| --------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| `radius`        | `4`                                             |\n| `fill`          | `var(--ts-chart-crosshair-marker-fill, Canvas)` |\n| `fillOpacity`   | None                                            |\n| `stroke`        | Focused point color, then guide or theme color  |\n| `strokeOpacity` | None                                            |\n| `strokeWidth`   | `2`                                             |\n| `opacity`       | None                                            |\n\nPass semantic axis types to `crosshair` when TypeScript should check label\nformatters independently from the surrounding definition:\n\n```ts\ncrosshair<Date, number>({\n  x: { label: { format: (value) => value.toISOString() } },\n  y: { label: { format: (value) => value.toFixed(1) } },\n})\n```\n\nMark order controls placement. Put `crosshair(...)` before the first ordinary\nmark for an underlay, or after it for an overlay. The default primary focus\nring still composes with the crosshair; set `focusRing: false` only when the\ncrosshair marker or other authored geometry deliberately replaces it.\n\nThe guide is `aria-hidden`. Pointer and keyboard users reach the same focus\nstate through the chart root, callbacks, and optional tooltip. `motion` on the\ncrosshair controls its keyed rules, bands, labels, and marker when the\ndefinition is mounted with the optional motion renderer. Active guide\nplacements remain visible through keyed scene updates so restored focus\nanimates from the previous geometry.\n\n## Controlled cursors\n\nImport the controller and host extension from the isolated cursor entry when\ninteraction state must be shared or set programmatically:\n\n```ts\nimport { createChartCursor, cursorHost } from '@tanstack/charts/cursor'\n\nconst sharedDate = createChartCursor<Date, number>()\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value' }),\n    crosshair({ x: { label: true }, y: false }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleUtc },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear },\n  },\n\n  focus: 'group-x',\n  cursor: {\n    use: cursorHost,\n    controller: sharedDate,\n    mode: 'focus',\n    match: 'x',\n    pin: true,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction createChartCursor<\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n>(\n  initialState: ChartCursorState<TXValue, TYValue> | null = null,\n): ChartCursorController<TXValue, TYValue>\n```\n\nEvery definition cursor binding accepts these common fields:\n\n| Option       | Default  | Meaning                                      |\n| ------------ | -------- | -------------------------------------------- |\n| `use`        | Required | Cursor host extension, normally `cursorHost` |\n| `controller` | Required | Observable structural cursor controller      |\n| `mode`       | Required | `focus` or `free` binding discriminator      |\n| `pin`        | `false`  | Enables activation to pin and dismiss        |\n\nMode-specific fields are:\n\n| Mode    | Option  | Default       | Meaning                                     |\n| ------- | ------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------- |\n| `focus` | `match` | `xy`          | Semantic axes shared between hosts          |\n| `free`  | `x`     | Scale inverse | Optional x-axis `valueAt` semantic override |\n| `free`  | `y`     | Scale inverse | Optional y-axis `valueAt` semantic override |\n\nA free cursor inverts each coordinate through that destination scene's\nresolved scale by default. Reversed ranges and responsive plot geometry need\nno copied scale. A missing or non-invertible scale requires an explicit\n`valueAt(context)` callback for that axis. Use the callback to replace default\ninversion with observed-value snapping, rounding, or another semantic mapping;\nreturn `undefined` for an intentionally coordinate-only axis.\n\n`createChartCursor` remains a three-method structural store. `cursorHost` opts\nthe binding into platform-neutral cursor policy without adding that policy to\ncharts that do not use cursors. Adapter and renderer authors can import the\nprojection, focus, presentation, and session helpers from\n`@tanstack/charts/cursor/host`; application code normally uses the token from\n`@tanstack/charts/cursor`.\n\n`ChartCursorExtensionToken` is the environment-neutral binding contract.\n`cursorHost` implements it as a `ChartCursorHostExtension`. Adapter authors\ncall `createChartCursorHostSession(binding)` to create an ownership-safe\n`ChartCursorHostSession` for one mounted binding.\n\nThat host entry uses `createFocusChartCursorState` and\n`createFreeChartCursorState` to publish state, then\n`resolveChartCursorPresentation` and `resolveChartCursorFocus` to project it\ninto each scene. `resolveChartPointerFocus` composes built-in axis focus with\npainted containment and returns `undefined` for default nearest focus so a host\ncan apply its spatial index or renderer geometry. `resolveChartFocusStrategy`\nconverts a built-in focus name to its grouping strategy.\n`resolveFocusPresentation` finally produces renderer-neutral underlay and\noverlay nodes. An empty array from `resolveChartPointerFocus` means an explicit\nstrategy found no target. Pass `scene.points` (the default) as its final\nargument to enable painted containment. A distinct array explicitly signals\nactive renderer presentation points and preserves anchor resolution while\nthose interpolated points are authoritative.\n\nUse the same controller in several browser or React Native definitions to\nsynchronize by semantic value rather than pixel position. In `focus` mode,\npointer, responder, keyboard, or accessibility focus publishes a\nvalue-anchored cursor. Each subscribing host maps the value through its own\nscales, resolves its own local point and focus group, and paints its crosshair\nand tooltip. `match` defaults to `xy`; choose `x` or `y` for an axis cursor.\nThe originating point's group is retained as the preferred series when more\nthan one local point matches. Optional `ChartCursorState.origin` carries\n`{ key, markId, datumIndex }` to retain the local point when equal values\nrepeat, including across facets. A unique `key` plus `markId` survives data\nreordering; `datumIndex` disambiguates duplicate keys. A consuming scene\nignores the tie-breaker when its key and mark do not exist, then resolves from\nthe portable semantic `value` and preferred `group`.\n\n`free` mode follows plot coordinates without resolving a datum:\n\n```ts\nconst freeCursor = createChartCursor<Date, number>()\n\nconst definition = defineChart(baseDefinition, {\n  cursor: {\n    use: cursorHost,\n    controller: freeCursor,\n    mode: 'free',\n    pin: true,\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe host publishes normalized plot coordinates so the free cursor survives a\nresponsive relayout, while resolved-scale inversion adds semantic values for\nlabels and application state. `valueAt` is an explicit replacement when the\ninteraction needs snapping or another precision policy. Programmatic state may\ninstead use `anchor: 'value'`,\n`anchor: 'normalized'`, or `anchor: 'scene'`. Only the anchor's coordinate\nspace is authoritative; the other fields are diagnostics from the host that\nlast emitted the state.\n\n`createChartCursor` returns `getState`, `setState`, and `subscribe`. Passing\n`null` clears the cursor, and `setState` accepts a previous-state updater.\nUnpinned host-owned state clears on pointer or responder cancellation, leave,\nand blur. Cleanup is ownership-safe: a host clears only the exact unpinned\nstate object it most recently published to that controller. State replaced by\nthe application or another host, and pinned state, survives that host's\ncleanup, rebinding, and unmount.\n\nWith `pin: true`, click or tap pins. Browser focus cursors toggle through Enter\nor Space and dismiss through Escape; React Native focus cursors expose the\nequivalent activate and escape accessibility actions. A pinned free cursor can\nbe toggled by another click or tap, or cleared programmatically.\n\nFree mode deliberately does not invent keyboard datum navigation. When its\nvalues are part of the reader's task, pair it with a labeled semantic control\nor status output. A focus-mode cursor inherits the chart's existing keyboard\nnavigation and accessible tooltip behavior.\n\nDefinition cursor bindings have browser and React Native host parity. DOM\nrenderer hosts publish pointer and keyboard interaction. React Native `Chart`\npublishes responder gestures and, for focus mode, accessibility navigation,\nactivation, and dismissal. Both hosts subscribe to programmatic updates and\nshared controllers. Free mode deliberately remains a coordinate gesture with\nno invented keyboard or accessibility datum order.\n\n## Controlled keyed selection\n\nImport controlled semantic selection from its exact subpath:\n\n```ts\nimport { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\nimport { keyedSelection, whenSelected } from '@tanstack/charts/selection'\n```\n\n`keyedSelection(options)` returns a `KeyedSelection` controller. Its\n`KeyedSelectionOptions` contain:\n\n| Option     | Meaning                                                                  |\n| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `selected` | Controlled signal holding a semantic key or `null`, with typed reasons   |\n| `key`      | Maps a datum and `{ point }` context to a semantic key or a nullish skip |\n\nThe key callback's second parameter is a typed `KeyedSelectionKeyContext`.\nControlled interaction state uses `ControlledSignal<TValue, TReason>`; its\nchange callback receives the proposed value and a\n`ControlledSignalChangeContext<TReason>` containing `{ reason }`.\n\nPlace that controller on `ChartDefinitionOptions.selection`. Pointer click,\nEnter, and Space pass the activated point to the controller. A blank-surface\nclick clears an existing selection. The controlled signal proposes a complete\nreplacement; Charts does not mutate its snapshot. Rebuild the definition with\nthe accepted value.\n\n`KeyedSelectionChange` is a discriminated union:\n\n- `{ type: 'select', value, point, source }` proposes a non-null semantic key;\n- `{ type: 'clear', value: null, point: null, source }` proposes clearing it.\n\n`source` is the `ChartSelectionSource` value `pointer` or `keyboard`. Selecting\nthe currently selected key still emits `select`, so application policy can\ndecide whether repeated activation is a no-op or another action. Returning\n`null` or `undefined` from `key` ignores activation for that point.\n\n`whenSelected(mark, selection)` returns the same ordinary mark filtered by the\ncontrolled key after domains resolve. The full mark still contributes scale\nchannels and domains. Its final geometry retains every fragment owned by a\nmatching point, while interaction metadata and points are removed. It is\ntherefore a decorative overlay and cannot create a duplicate focus or\nactivation target. Duplicate semantic keys intentionally paint every match.\n\nSelection does not replace focus, tooltip pinning, or application UI. A linked\nHTML table, live status, clear action, and persistence remain outside the\ndefinition and can update the same controlled key.\n\n## Continuous cursor\n\nImport `continuousCursor` from `@tanstack/charts/interaction/cursor` and place\nit in `ChartDefinitionOptions.controls`. Its `position` is a controlled\n`ContinuousCursorPosition<TXValue, TYValue> | null`. Both values must be\nnumbers or dates backed by invertible scales.\n\n`ContinuousCursorChange` reports one of:\n\n- `preview` for pointer or touch movement, leave, and cancellation;\n- `commit` when click or tap proposes a pinned position; or\n- `clear` when a pinned cursor is toggled off or Escape is pressed.\n\nEvery reason includes the proposed `value`, the controlled `origin`, a\n`source`, and a more specific `cause`. Rebuild the definition with an accepted\ncommit or clear. Pointer previews remain host-local while the controlled value\nis `null`, avoiding a chart-scene render for each movement.\n\n`xRule`, `yRule`, and `marker` configure renderer-neutral guide geometry and\nare enabled by default. `xLabel` and `yLabel` are opt-in and accept semantic\nformatters plus side, offset, padding, background, text, stroke, and typography\noptions. The behavior resolves all geometry against the final plot and scale\nranges.\n\nSVG and Canvas DOM hosts mount the same contained pointer overlay. Static SVG\nand React Native retain only the guide for an accepted non-null position. The\noverlay is presentation-only, so applications should pair it with semantic\nsliders or inputs, status text, and any persistence or rounding policy.\n\nThis differs from `focusGuideX` and `focusGuideY`: focus guides retarget to a\ndatum-owned chart point and participate in mark motion, while\n`continuousCursor` tracks an unsnapped value pair without creating a focus or\nselection point.\n\nThe public cursor types divide state, event, and presentation concerns:\n\n| Type                                        | Contract                                                           |\n| ------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `ContinuousCursorValue`                     | A finite `number` or valid `Date`                                  |\n| `ContinuousCursorPosition<TX, TY>`          | The semantic x/y pair                                              |\n| `ContinuousCursorPointerSource`             | Pointer or touch preview/commit source                             |\n| `ContinuousCursorSource`                    | Pointer, touch, or keyboard clear source                           |\n| `ContinuousCursorChange<TX, TY>`            | Preview, commit, and clear reason union                            |\n| `ContinuousCursorRuleOptions`               | Rule stroke, opacity, width, dash, and cap                         |\n| `ContinuousCursorMarkerOptions`             | Marker radius, fill, stroke, and opacity                           |\n| `ContinuousCursorLabelOptions<TValue>`      | Formatter, side, spacing, box, text, stroke, and typography        |\n| `ContinuousCursorOptions<TXValue, TYValue>` | Controlled position plus x/y rule, marker, and label configuration |\n\n## Horizontal brush\n\nImport `brushX` from `@tanstack/charts/interaction/brush` and place it in\n`ChartDefinitionOptions.controls`. Its `range` is a controlled\n`BrushRange<TValue>` with inclusive semantic `start` and `end` values.\n\n`BrushXChange` reports `preview`, `commit`, or `cancel`, the proposed and\norigin ranges, the pointer or keyboard source, and whether the selection,\nstart, end, or a new blank region was manipulated. Explicit `values` determine\norder, snapping, slider indices, and keyboard steps. String values require\nthem. Number and Date values may instead use scale inversion only when\n`keyboard: false`.\n\n`BrushXValuesOptions<TValue>` is the candidate-backed form.\n`format` supplies each keyboard handle's value text. Its\n`BrushXFormatContext` names the `start` or `end` handle, so values that mark\nboundaries between items, such as bar edges, can name the first item after the\nstart handle and the last item before the end handle.\n`BrushXContinuousOptions<number | Date>` is the invertible, pointer-only form.\n`BrushXSource` distinguishes pointer and keyboard commits, while\n`BrushXTarget` identifies the selection, start handle, end handle, or newly\ndrawn region.\n\nThe behavior resolves against the final x scale and plot bounds. SVG and\nCanvas DOM hosts mount the same D3-backed overlay and contain its events before\nnormal chart focus or selection. Static SVG and React Native paint the\nrenderer-neutral range and handles without interactive host controls. The\napplication still owns fixed-window expansion, validation, linked-view layout,\nstatus text, persistence, and any native semantic control.\n\n## Horizontal scale handle\n\nImport `handleX` from `@tanstack/charts/interaction/handle` and place it in\n`ChartDefinitionOptions.controls`. It binds one controlled x value to an\nexplicit ordered candidate list. The controlled value must equal one of those\ncandidates.\n\n`HandleXOptions<TXValue, TYValue>` contains:\n\n| Option        | Contract                                                               |\n| ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `value`       | `ControlledSignal<TXValue, HandleXChange<TXValue>>`                    |\n| `values`      | Ordered candidates for scale positioning, snapping, and keyboard steps |\n| `cross`       | `{ edge: 'top' \\| 'bottom', offset? }` or `{ value: TYValue }`         |\n| `trackStyle`  | Scene style for the horizontal candidate track                         |\n| `ruleStyle`   | Scene style for the vertical rule, or `false`                          |\n| `handleStyle` | Scene style for the current-value handle                               |\n| `hitSize`     | Pointer target height and end padding; defaults to `44`                |\n| `ariaLabel`   | Slider name; defaults to `Horizontal value`                            |\n| `format`      | Formats the semantic value for `aria-valuetext`                        |\n| `keyboard`    | Enables the slider keyboard task; defaults to `true`                   |\n| `id`          | Stable behavior and host-control identity                              |\n\nAn edge cross places the track outside the corresponding plot edge by its\noptional offset. A value cross maps the supplied semantic y value through the\nfinal y scale and places the track inside the plot. For an edge cross, the\nvertical rule spans from the opposite plot edge to the track. For a value\ncross, it spans from the plot top to that value.\n\n`HandleXCross<TValue>` names this edge-or-value union.\n`HandleXPointerSource` is `pointer | touch`; `HandleXSource` adds `keyboard`\nfor committed and canceled changes.\n\n`HandleXChange<TValue>` is a discriminated union. Pointer and touch gestures\npropose `preview`, then `commit` on release or `cancel` with the origin value.\nArrow keys move one candidate, Home and End move to the first and last\ncandidate, and every keyboard change commits immediately. Each reason carries\nthe proposed `value`, gesture `origin`, and pointer, touch, or keyboard\n`source`.\n\nThe behavior resolves against the final x and optional y scales. SVG and\nCanvas DOM hosts mount the same contained slider overlay and preserve an\nactive gesture across accepted controlled updates. Static SVG and React Native\npaint the accepted track, rule, and handle without an interactive control.\nApplications retain playback timing, status, persistence, and native semantic\ncontrols.\n\n## Horizontal zoom\n\nImport `zoomX` from `@tanstack/charts/interaction/zoom` and place it in\n`ChartDefinitionOptions.controls`. Its `window` is a controlled\n`ZoomXWindow<TValue>` with semantic `start` and `end` values. `TValue` must be\na number or Date backed by an invertible x scale.\n\n`extent` defines the complete allowed x domain. `scaleExtent` defaults to\n`[1, Infinity]`; its first value must be `1`, and its second value sets the\nmaximum zoom factor relative to the extent. The configured x scale must use\nthe controlled window as its domain; accepted proposals rebuild the definition\nwith that same window.\n\nThe behavior resolves against the final x scale and plot bounds. Its contained\nSVG or Canvas host control owns pointer-anchored vertical-wheel zoom, drag and\nhorizontal-wheel pan, pointer and touch input, delta-mode normalization,\nclamping, cancellation, and teardown. `wheelActivation` defaults to `focus`, so\nwheel input is captured only while the plot control is focused. Use `modifier`\nto capture Control+wheel or Command+wheel under the pointer without prior focus\nwhile every unmodified wheel continues to scroll the page. Use `always` only\nwhen the chart should capture every wheel over its plot. Plus and minus zoom\naround the center, arrow keys pan, Home proposes the full extent, and Escape\ncancels an active gesture.\n\n`ZoomXChange` reports `preview`, `commit`, or `cancel`; the proposed `value`,\nthe gesture `origin`, a `zoom`, `pan`, or `reset` action, and a wheel, pointer,\ntouch, or keyboard source. Wheel streams and direct manipulation preview before\ntheir terminal event. Keyboard changes commit immediately. A cancel proposes\nthe origin window.\n\nSet `keyboard: false` only when an application supplies equivalent semantic\ncontrols. `ariaLabel` names the plot control, `ariaDescription` can replace its\ngenerated instructions, `format` formats values in those instructions, and\n`onActiveChange` observes focus without moving accepted state into Charts.\n\nCharts does not mutate the controlled snapshot. Keep visible-row filtering or\nclipping, y-domain policy, status, reset and recovery controls, follow-latest\nbehavior, and persistence in the application. Static SVG and React Native\nrender the accepted configured window but provide no zoom host control.\n\nThe public zoom types are:\n\n| Type                   | Contract                                                                 |\n| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `ZoomXValue`           | A finite `number` or valid `Date`                                        |\n| `ZoomXWindow<TValue>`  | Semantic `start` and `end` values                                        |\n| `ZoomXSource`          | Wheel, pointer, touch, or keyboard origin                                |\n| `ZoomXAction`          | Zoom, pan, or reset action                                               |\n| `ZoomXWheelActivation` | Focus, modifier-gated, or unconditional wheel capture                    |\n| `ZoomXChange<TValue>`  | Preview, commit, and cancel reason union                                 |\n| `ZoomXOptions<TValue>` | Controlled window, extent, limits, wheel, accessibility, and key options |\n\n## Disabling chart-owned focus\n\nSet `focus: false` to disable chart-owned pointer and keyboard point focus. The\nscene keeps its semantic points but omits the generated default focus layer,\nand the DOM host forces the chart surface out of the tab order. Explicit\nfocus-only marks remain available to custom renderers and programmatic paint.\n\n```ts\nimport { focusDisabled } from '@tanstack/charts/focus/disabled'\n```\n\n`focusDisabled` resolves, groups, and navigates to no points. Use it when an\napplication owns gestures, selection paint, accessibility, and task semantics\noutside the native resolver but still needs the rendered focus layer. Set\ndefinition `keyboard: false` and omit its `tooltip` as appropriate for that\napplication-owned interaction.\n\n## Custom focus strategies\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartFocusStrategy<\n  TDatum = unknown,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n> {\n  resolve(\n    points: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[],\n    context: ChartFocusResolveContext,\n  ): readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[]\n\n  group(\n    points: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[],\n    context: ChartFocusGroupContext<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>,\n  ): readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[]\n\n  navigation(\n    points: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[],\n  ): readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[]\n}\n```\n\n`ChartFocusResolveContext` contains scene-pixel `x`, `y`, and `maxDistance`.\n`resolve` returns the primary point first. `ChartFocusGroupContext` contains\nthe point restored or reached through keyboard navigation. `navigation`\nreturns the ordered keyboard task set.\n\n`ChartFocusMode` accepts a `ChartFocusPreset` string or a\n`ChartFocusStrategy`.\n\n## Tooltips\n\nImport `tooltip` from `@tanstack/charts/tooltip` and place it on the definition\nfor the native accessible tooltip. The default is a structured label-value\ntable. Grouped focus adds a shared-axis heading and one color swatch and value\nrow per series. Visible axis labels are reused. Numbers use browser locale\nformatting; dates use stable UTC ISO formatting.\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartTooltipOptions<\n  TDatum = unknown,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n> {\n  className?: string\n  portal?: ChartTooltipPortalInput\n  items?: readonly ChartTooltipItem<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[]\n  sort?: ChartTooltipSort<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>\n  anchor?: ChartTooltipAnchor<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>\n  placement?: 'auto' | ChartTooltipPlacement | readonly ChartTooltipPlacement[]\n  offset?: number\n  content?: (\n    points: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[],\n    context: ChartTooltipContentContext,\n  ) => ChartTooltipContent\n  format?: (\n    point: ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>,\n    context: ChartTooltipContentContext,\n  ) => string\n  formatGroup?: (\n    points: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[],\n    context: ChartTooltipContentContext,\n  ) => string\n  sticky?: boolean\n  visibility?: 'focus' | 'pinned'\n}\n```\n\n| Option        | Default        | Meaning                                               |\n| ------------- | -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n| `className`   | None           | Class appended after `ts-chart-tooltip`               |\n| `portal`      | None           | Optional top-layer or fixed-position transport        |\n| `items`       | Automatic x/y  | Ordered rows for a single focused point               |\n| `sort`        | `visual`       | Grouped row order                                     |\n| `anchor`      | `point`        | Preset, per-axis coordinates, or coordinate resolver  |\n| `placement`   | `auto`         | Fixed or ordered fallback box placements              |\n| `offset`      | `10`           | Scene-pixel gap between anchor and box                |\n| `content`     | Automatic rows | Returns a safe title and structured rows              |\n| `format`      | None           | Replaces content with primary-point text              |\n| `formatGroup` | None           | Replaces content with focused-group text              |\n| `sticky`      | `true`         | Enables activation-to-pin and text selection          |\n| `visibility`  | `focus`        | Shows on focus or only after activation with `pinned` |\n\nFormatting precedence is `content`, `formatGroup`, `format`, then the default.\nThe text formatters do not parse HTML, and newlines are preserved. `className`\nis appended to `ts-chart-tooltip`.\n\nThe default DOM surface inherits these optional CSS variables from the chart\ncontainer while retaining its built-in fallback values. A portaled Popover\nkeeps that ancestry. If the portal fallback moves the tooltip under\n`ownerDocument.body`, chart-container-only variables no longer inherit; set\nthem on the tooltip class or a shared document ancestor instead:\n\n| Variable                           | Controls                    |\n| ---------------------------------- | --------------------------- |\n| `--ts-chart-tooltip-background`    | Surface background          |\n| `--ts-chart-tooltip-color`         | Text color                  |\n| `--ts-chart-tooltip-border`        | Complete border declaration |\n| `--ts-chart-tooltip-border-radius` | Corner radius               |\n| `--ts-chart-tooltip-shadow`        | Box shadow                  |\n| `--ts-chart-tooltip-max-width`     | Maximum width               |\n| `--ts-chart-tooltip-padding`       | Inner spacing               |\n| `--ts-chart-tooltip-font`          | Complete font shorthand     |\n\nUse `className` for content structure or additional selectors. Use the\nvariables for ordinary surface theming without specificity overrides.\n\n`ChartTooltipContentContext.pinned` is `false` during transient inspection and\n`true` after activation. `content`, `format`, `formatGroup`, and item `text`\nreceive the same context, so either structured or plaintext content can reveal\nadditional detail when pinned.\n\n### Ordered point items\n\n`items` is an ordered single-point row list. Use `x`, `y`, and `group`\nshorthands, a configured channel, a scalar datum field, or derived text:\n\n```ts\nconst detailedDefinition = defineChart(definition, {\n  tooltip: {\n    use: tooltip,\n    items: [\n      {\n        channel: 'y',\n        label: 'Revenue',\n        text: (point) => currency(point.yValue),\n      },\n      {\n        field: 'volume',\n        label: 'Volume',\n        text: (point) => compact(point.datum.volume),\n      },\n      {\n        id: 'change',\n        label: 'Change',\n        text: (point) =>\n          point.datum.change == null ? null : percent(point.datum.change),\n      },\n      'x',\n      'group',\n    ],\n  },\n})\n```\n\nArray order is row order. A nullish datum field or nullish `text` result omits\nthe row. Adding `group` to `items` renders it as a row instead of the automatic\nsingle-point title. In grouped focus, the shared-axis item supplies the heading\nlabel and text, the opposite-axis item formats values, and the group item\nformats series names. `sort` orders those generated series rows. Additional\ngrouped structure belongs in `content`.\n\n`sort` accepts `visual`, `color-domain`, `focus`, or a typed point comparator.\nVisual order follows the marks across the screen: top-to-bottom for an x-group\nand left-to-right for a y-group.\n\n### Anchor and placement\n\n`anchor` controls the scene coordinate followed by the box:\n\n- `point` follows the primary focused point.\n- `pointer` follows the current pointer and falls back to the point for\n  keyboard focus.\n- `group-center` uses the center of the focused points' bounding box.\n- `{ x, y }` chooses each coordinate from point, pointer, semantic value,\n  group center, or a plot edge.\n- A resolver receives the focused points plus `{ focus, pointer, plot,\nsurface, scales }` and returns scene coordinates. A nullish or non-finite\n  result falls back to the primary point.\n\n`placement` accepts `auto`, one placement, or an ordered fallback list. The\nplacements are `top`, `top-right`, `right`, `bottom-right`, `bottom`,\n`bottom-left`, `left`, and `top-left`. A single placement is fixed and shifted\ninside the surface. A list uses the first placement that fits; if none fits,\nit uses the least-overflowing candidate and shifts it inside. `auto` uses\n`top`, `bottom`, `right`, then `left`.\n\n```ts\nconst groupedDefinition = defineChart(definition, {\n  tooltip: {\n    use: tooltip,\n    anchor: { x: 'plot-center', y: 'plot-top' },\n    placement: ['top', 'right', 'left', 'bottom'],\n    offset: 12,\n  },\n})\n```\n\nImport `portal` from `@tanstack/charts/tooltip/portal` and assign it to the\ntooltip's `portal` option when an ancestor clips overflow or creates an\nincompatible stacking context. The host opens the tooltip as a manual Popover\nin the browser top layer where supported, while retaining its chart DOM\nancestry. If Popover is unavailable or fails, it moves the tooltip directly\nunder the chart's `ownerDocument` body with fixed high-stack positioning. Both\npaths map the scene anchor to viewport coordinates, reposition during scroll,\nresize, and content resize, and collide against the viewport instead of the\nchart box.\n\nClicking, Enter, or Space pins the tooltip. The next activation unpins it.\nPressing the pointer outside the chart and tooltip, or pressing `Escape`,\nunpins and clears focus. Set `sticky: false` to disable pinning. A display-only\ntooltip has `role=\"status\"` and `aria-live=\"polite\"`.\n\nSet `visibility: 'pinned'` for click-or-keyboard detail that should not paint a\ntransient shell. Focus and inline mark states still update before activation;\nthe tooltip element and adapter body mount only while pinned.\n\n`content` supports display-only rows. Every framework adapter can compose\nnative content around those rows while preserving the definition's ordering,\nanchor, placement, portal, and pinning behavior. A pinned custom body has\nnon-modal dialog semantics.\n\n## Callbacks\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartInteractionCallbacks {\n  onFocusChange?: (point: ChartPoint | null) => void\n  onFocusGroupChange?: (points: readonly ChartPoint[]) => void\n  onSelect?: (point: ChartPoint | null) => void\n}\n```\n\nDefinitions infer callback datum and semantic x/y value types without adapter\ngenerics. Focus callbacks run only when the primary focus key changes, except\nthat a scene rebuild with an existing focused key reports the point with its\nnew coordinates and datum.\n\n`onSelect` reports mouse clicks and keyboard activation. A click with no point\nreports `null`; Enter and Space do nothing until a point is focused. When a\ndefinition has a selection controller, it receives the activation before the\nlegacy callback.\n\n## Spatial indexes\n\nThe default lookup scans the cached scene targets linearly. Dense charts can\ninject an index:\n\n```ts\ntype ChartSpatialIndexFactory<\n  TDatum = unknown,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n> = (\n  points: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[],\n  context: ChartSpatialIndexFactoryContext<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>,\n) => ChartSpatialIndex<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>\n```\n\nThe host rebuilds the index when the scene or definition changes. The index\nowns its search algorithm and must apply `maxDistance`. Point-only factories\ncan ignore the second argument; geometry-aware indexes can traverse\n`context.scene` and use primitive bounds as their acceleration layer.\nUse the granular spatial primitive appropriate to the data; the boundary is\ndescribed in [Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md).\n\nSupplying an index also replaces default primitive containment and affinity\nranking; the host does not add a linear safety scan after an indexed query. An\nindex that wants identical geometry semantics should index scene-primitive\nbounds and perform exact shape checks on its candidates.\n\nA custom `focus` strategy takes precedence over `spatialIndex` for pointer\nresolution.\n\n## Application-owned gestures\n\nDragging, scrolling, custom crosshair overlays, and freeform range or lasso\nselections can listen on a wrapper or use `onRender` to attach application\nbehavior to the default SVG or complete surface. Use `handleX` for one ordered\nscale value, `brushX` for a normal horizontal semantic range, and `zoomX` for a\nnormal controlled x window. For custom gestures, keep semantic state outside\nthe scene and update a responsive definition by replacing its identity.\n\nUse a definition cursor binding for snapped or free crosshairs. Keep other\nsemantic state outside the scene.\n\nFor application-timed datum inspection, use the stable controller instead of\nreimplementing coordinate conversion and focus:\n\n```ts\nconst position = interaction.clientToScene(event.clientX, event.clientY)\nconst target = interaction.resolvePointer(event.clientX, event.clientY)\ninteraction.setControlledFocus(target)\ninteraction.setControlledFocus(null)\n```\n\nThe resolution contains the scene position, primary point, and focus group.\nPassing that resolution to `setControlledFocus` infers `source: 'pointer'` and\nre-resolves its held position after scene and presentation updates. Passing a\nraw `ChartPoint` defaults to `source: 'programmatic'`; either source can be\noverridden explicitly. Controlled focus is not cleared by unrelated\npointer-leave or focus-out events. Pass `{ pinned: true }` to make an enabled\nsticky tooltip interactive.\n\nClean up application listeners before the next attachment or unmount.\nFor a completely independent renderer or interaction layer, use the scene and\nextension contracts in [Custom extensions](./custom-extensions.md).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Focus and Interaction\ndescription: Configure pointer focus, grouped focus, crosshairs, controlled cursors, keyboard navigation, built-in tooltips, selection, and spatial indexes.\n---\n\nThe DOM hosts, framework adapters, and React Native `Chart` provide point-level\ninteraction from each mark's emitted `ChartPoint` values and rendered scene\nprimitives. The defaults cover geometry-aware pointer or responder focus,\nlinear keyboard or accessibility navigation, activation, and an optional\nbuilt-in tooltip. Definitions own these policies; adapters mount them and report\nevents.\n\n## Default behavior\n\nWith no custom focus strategy:\n\n- pointer movement finds the nearest point within `maxFocusDistance`\n- pointer leave or cancellation clears unpinned focus\n- the SVG uses `tabIndex` (`0` by default) when `keyboard` is enabled\n- focusing the SVG selects the first point in the keyboard task order\n- arrow keys move through points sorted by pixel x, then pixel y\n- `Home` and `End` move to the first and last point\n- `Enter` and Space toggle an enabled sticky tooltip and call `onSelect` for\n  the focused point\n- pressing the pointer outside both the chart and the tooltip dismisses a pinned tooltip\n- a configured selection controller receives the same focused point before\n  `onSelect`\n- a click focuses and selects the nearest point, or selects `null` on the\n  blank surface\n- the renderer's focus ring follows the primary point\n\n`maxFocusDistance` defaults to `48` scene pixels. Set `tabIndex` to control\nnormal tab-order participation while keeping keyboard handling enabled. Set\n`keyboard: false` to remove keyboard navigation and force tab index `-1`.\nAuthored `whenFocused` marks compose with the primary-point ring. Set\ndefinition `focusRing: false` only when authored focus geometry replaces that\nindicator.\n\n### Built-in focus ring\n\nPass an options object to change the built-in ring without targeting generated\nSVG:\n\n```ts\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks,\n  scales,\n  focusRing: {\n    radius: 4,\n    strokeWidth: 2,\n    fill: '#ffffff',\n  },\n})\n```\n\n| Option        | Default                                   |\n| ------------- | ----------------------------------------- |\n| `radius`      | `5`                                       |\n| `strokeWidth` | `2.5`                                     |\n| `fill`        | `var(--ts-chart-focus-fill, Canvas)`      |\n| `stroke`      | The focused point's resolved series color |\n\nThe object form still enables the ring. `true` keeps every default and `false`\nremoves only the built-in ring. Set the same value on `theme.focusRing` to\nprovide a theme default. An explicitly supplied definition-level `focusRing`\nvalue takes precedence, including `true` or `false`. Composed views and facet\ncells share the outer chart's focus layer, so configure their focus ring on\nthe outer definition or theme. Their child specs reject both `focusRing` and\n`theme.focusRing`. The definition compiles this presentation into ordinary\nscene dots, so SVG, Canvas, React Native, facets, polar marks, custom mark\nrenderers, and the optional motion renderer share the same values. The ring\nremains hidden from the accessibility tree; the chart root continues to report\nfocus and values. `radius` and `strokeWidth` accept finite nonnegative numbers,\nincluding zero. Invalid values use their defaults.\n\nUse `focusGuideX` or `focusGuideY` from `@tanstack/charts/focus/guide` for\ndatum-bound rules, markers, and axis labels. Their active geometry uses stable\nstructural keys, so the optional motion renderer can animate rapid focus\nretargets without an application SVG or frame loop. See\n[Focus guide marks](./marks/focus-guide.md).\n\nSet `pointer: false` when the application owns the pointer gesture but still\nwants chart focus, focus marks, and tooltips. Automatic pointer move, leave,\nand click handling stops; keyboard navigation remains independent. Resolve and\npaint application-owned focus through `host.interaction` or the interaction\ncontroller reported by `onRender`.\n\n### Interaction geometry\n\nWhen neither `focus` nor `spatialIndex` is supplied, pointer resolution has two\nstages:\n\n1. The topmost interactive scene primitive containing the pointer wins. The\n   resolver traverses the final scene in reverse paint order and applies nested\n   translations and clips.\n2. If no primitive contains the pointer, its `interaction.affinity` ranks the\n   fallback. `x` and `y` compare distance from that axis boundary first and use\n   complete geometry distance to break ties; `xy` compares complete geometry\n   distance; `geometry` has no off-shape fallback.\n\n`maxFocusDistance` applies to that primary boundary distance, not necessarily\nto the point used as the tooltip and keyboard anchor. A semantic point that is\nnot attached to a scene primitive retains anchor-based two-dimensional\ndistance.\n\nThe primitive is the geometry source of truth: `rect` includes its rounded\ncorners, `dot` uses its radius, `area` uses its polygon, and `polyline` and\n`rule` use their stroked paths. Its `interaction` attaches either one semantic\npoint or the ordered points represented by a continuous primitive. Groups and\nlabels cannot carry interaction metadata.\n\nBuilt-in marks attach these natural defaults:\n\n| Mark                     | Scene primitive       | Fallback |\n| ------------------------ | --------------------- | -------- |\n| `barY`                   | Rounded rectangle     | `x`      |\n| `barX`                   | Rounded rectangle     | `y`      |\n| `lineY`                  | Stroked polyline      | `x`      |\n| `areaY`                  | Filled area           | `x`      |\n| `areaX`                  | Filled area           | `y`      |\n| `rect`, `dot`, `hexagon` | Rect, circle, polygon | `xy`     |\n| `bandX`                  | Rounded rectangle     | `x`      |\n| `bandY`                  | Rounded rectangle     | `y`      |\n\nFacet layout rewrites the primitive's attached point references while leaving\nthe primitive in local coordinates. The resolver therefore observes the same\npost-layout translations and clips as SVG and Canvas instead of maintaining a\nsecond geometry copy. Inline mark states similarly return a resolved scene to\nthe host; during a transition, pointer selection intentionally follows that\ndestination scene rather than interpolating a second hit-test scene.\n\nWith an active axis viewport, the host calls\n`viewportInteractionPoints(scene, presentationPoints)` before pointer,\nkeyboard, custom-focus, or spatial-index resolution. Off-window anchors from\nclipped viewport content remain in `scene.points` but are not focus candidates;\nfixed-ownership mark points remain eligible. Direct scene consumers can pass\nthat filtered list as the optional final argument to\n`findNearestPoint(scene, x, y, maxDistance, points)` so primitive hit testing\nuses the same candidate set.\n\nFor curved `polyline` and `area` nodes, the current resolver uses the\nprimitive's structured point geometry. Exact picking against an optional\nauthored SVG path string remains a separate refinement.\n\nBuilt-in axis focus modes compose painted containment with axis snapping. When\nthe pointer is inside an interactive primitive, the topmost painted mark seeds\nthe primary point. Outside painted geometry, the configured axis mode uses its\nnormal nearest-axis fallback. A custom strategy replaces this host behavior.\n\n## Focus modes\n\nUse a preset for built-in focus behavior:\n\n```ts\nimport { tooltip } from '@tanstack/charts/tooltip'\n\nconst groupedDownloads = defineChart(definition, {\n  focus: 'group-x',\n  tooltip,\n})\n```\n\n| Preset      | Pointer resolution                                                     | Group returned to callbacks and tooltip                               | Keyboard navigation                     |\n| ----------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |\n| `nearest`   | Nearest painted geometry or point in two dimensions                    | Primary point only                                                    | Every point                             |\n| `nearest-x` | Containing painted mark; otherwise nearest x, then nearest y           | Primary point only                                                    | Every point                             |\n| `nearest-y` | Containing painted mark; otherwise nearest y, then nearest x           | Primary point only                                                    | Every point                             |\n| `group-x`   | Containing painted mark; otherwise nearest x, then nearest y at that x | One point per group sharing the semantic x value; primary point first | One representative per semantic x value |\n| `group-y`   | Containing painted mark; otherwise nearest y, then nearest x at that y | One point per group sharing the semantic y value; primary point first | One representative per semantic y value |\n\nGrouping compares semantic values, including dates by timestamp. Duplicate\npoints with the same `group` value are reduced to one member in grouped focus.\n\nThe equivalent `focusGroupX`, `focusGroupY`, `focusNearestX`, and `focusNearestY`\nstrategy objects remain available from `@tanstack/charts/focus` for composition\nor direct strategy use. The exact exported objects receive the same host-level\ncontainment behavior as their presets. A strategy that wraps or copies one of\nthem is custom and owns its complete pointer resolution.\n\n`focusGroupAngle` is available from `@tanstack/charts/polar`. It resolves the\nnearest radial ray, groups points with the same semantic angle value, and\norders keyboard tasks by angle. Use it for grouped radar, polar-line, and\nradial-dot tooltips. Painted `radialArc` geometry already participates in\ndefault nearest focus.\n\n## Crosshair guides\n\n`crosshair` is a data-less presentation mark. It follows the chart's resolved\nfocus state without adding scale values or pointer targets:\n\n```ts\nimport { crosshair, defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value', color: 'series' }),\n    crosshair({\n      x: { label: true },\n      y: false,\n      strokeDasharray: '4 4',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleUtc },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear },\n  },\n\n  focus: 'group-x',\n  maxFocusDistance: Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY,\n})\n```\n\nThe x guide is vertical and the y guide is horizontal. Both default to\nenabled; labels and the intersection marker default to disabled. Shared rule\noptions apply to both axes, and an axis object can override them. A label uses\nthe matching resolved tick label when available, falls back to the semantic\nvalue, and accepts a `format` callback. Labels are clamped to the chart surface\nand rendered with a configurable halo. Rules and the optional marker are\nclipped to the plot. For difference intervals such as stacked bars and areas,\nthe label formats the plotted `x2` or `y2` endpoint so it matches the guide;\ntooltip formatting still reports the interval difference.\n\n`crosshair<TXValue, TYValue>(options = {})` accepts these top-level options:\n\n| Option   | Default                   | Meaning                                      |\n| -------- | ------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |\n| `id`     | `crosshair-${markIndex}`  | Stable mark and guide identity               |\n| `x`      | `true`                    | Vertical rule or categorical band            |\n| `y`      | `true`                    | Horizontal rule or categorical band          |\n| `marker` | `false`                   | Marker at the resolved x/y intersection      |\n| `motion` | No mark-specific override | Optional guide spring or tween configuration |\n\nThe shared rule options apply to both axes. The same fields on an axis object\noverride the shared value for that axis:\n\n| Rule option       | Default          | Meaning                            |\n| ----------------- | ---------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| `stroke`          | Chart foreground | Rule color                         |\n| `strokeOpacity`   | `0.35`           | Rule stroke opacity                |\n| `strokeWidth`     | `1`              | Nonnegative rule width             |\n| `strokeDasharray` | None             | SVG-compatible stroke dash pattern |\n\nAn x or y axis object adds two options:\n\n| Axis option | Default | Meaning                                       |\n| ----------- | ------- | --------------------------------------------- |\n| `label`     | `false` | `true` for defaults or a label options object |\n| `band`      | `false` | `true` for defaults or a band options object  |\n\nOn a categorical axis, `band` replaces that axis rule with a plot-spanning\ncursor band centered on the focused value:\n\n```ts\nmarks: [\n  crosshair({\n    x: {\n      band: {\n        inset: 0,\n        radius: 3,\n        fill: '#64748b',\n        fillOpacity: 0.16,\n      },\n      label: true,\n    },\n    y: false,\n  }),\n  barY(rows, {\n    x: 'period',\n    y: 'value',\n    color: 'series',\n    inset: 4,\n  }),\n  crosshair({\n    x: false,\n    y: { strokeDasharray: '4 4', label: true },\n  }),\n]\n```\n\nThe first guide is an underlay because it precedes the bars; the second is an\noverlay. With a bar inset of 4 and band inset of 0, the cursor extends exactly\n4 pixels beyond each bar edge. Its x label shows the focused period; the y rule\nlabel shows the focused stack endpoint. Use separate crosshair marks whenever\naxes need different placement.\n\n| Band option     | Default          | Meaning                                                                  |\n| --------------- | ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `inset`         | `0`              | Pixels removed from both scale-band edges; negative values create outset |\n| `radius`        | None             | Corner radius                                                            |\n| `fill`          | Chart foreground | Fill color                                                               |\n| `fillOpacity`   | `0.12`           | Fill opacity                                                             |\n| `stroke`        | None             | Optional outline color                                                   |\n| `strokeOpacity` | None             | Optional outline opacity                                                 |\n| `strokeWidth`   | None             | Optional nonnegative outline width                                       |\n| `opacity`       | None             | Opacity applied to the complete band                                     |\n\n`band: true` uses those defaults. Band geometry uses the resolved scale\nbandwidth, spans the plot in the other direction, and remains clipped to the\nplot. A continuous scale or any other axis with zero bandwidth emits no band.\nThe axis label can still be enabled because `band` replaces only the rule.\n\nLabel options control formatting and paint outside the plot:\n\n| Label option    | Default                                        |\n| --------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |\n| `format`        | Matching scale tick label, then semantic value |\n| `offset`        | `8`                                            |\n| `fill`          | Chart foreground                               |\n| `fillOpacity`   | None                                           |\n| `stroke`        | `var(--ts-chart-crosshair-label-halo, Canvas)` |\n| `strokeOpacity` | None                                           |\n| `strokeWidth`   | `3`                                            |\n| `opacity`       | None                                           |\n| `fontSize`      | `11`                                           |\n| `fontWeight`    | None                                           |\n\nMarker options control the resolved x/y intersection marker:\n\n| Marker option   | Default                                         |\n| --------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| `radius`        | `4`                                             |\n| `fill`          | `var(--ts-chart-crosshair-marker-fill, Canvas)` |\n| `fillOpacity`   | None                                            |\n| `stroke`        | Focused point color, then guide or theme color  |\n| `strokeOpacity` | None                                            |\n| `strokeWidth`   | `2`                                             |\n| `opacity`       | None                                            |\n\nPass semantic axis types to `crosshair` when TypeScript should check label\nformatters independently from the surrounding definition:\n\n```ts\ncrosshair<Date, number>({\n  x: { label: { format: (value) => value.toISOString() } },\n  y: { label: { format: (value) => value.toFixed(1) } },\n})\n```\n\nMark order controls placement. Put `crosshair(...)` before the first ordinary\nmark for an underlay, or after it for an overlay. The default primary focus\nring still composes with the crosshair; set `focusRing: false` only when the\ncrosshair marker or other authored geometry deliberately replaces it.\n\nThe guide is `aria-hidden`. Pointer and keyboard users reach the same focus\nstate through the chart root, callbacks, and optional tooltip. `motion` on the\ncrosshair controls its keyed rules, bands, labels, and marker when the\ndefinition is mounted with the optional motion renderer. Active guide\nplacements remain visible through keyed scene updates so restored focus\nanimates from the previous geometry.\n\n## Controlled cursors\n\nImport the controller and host extension from the isolated cursor entry when\ninteraction state must be shared or set programmatically:\n\n```ts\nimport { createChartCursor, cursorHost } from '@tanstack/charts/cursor'\n\nconst sharedDate = createChartCursor<Date, number>()\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value' }),\n    crosshair({ x: { label: true }, y: false }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleUtc },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear },\n  },\n\n  focus: 'group-x',\n  cursor: {\n    use: cursorHost,\n    controller: sharedDate,\n    mode: 'focus',\n    match: 'x',\n    pin: true,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction createChartCursor<\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n>(\n  initialState: ChartCursorState<TXValue, TYValue> | null = null,\n): ChartCursorController<TXValue, TYValue>\n```\n\nEvery definition cursor binding accepts these common fields:\n\n| Option       | Default  | Meaning                                      |\n| ------------ | -------- | -------------------------------------------- |\n| `use`        | Required | Cursor host extension, normally `cursorHost` |\n| `controller` | Required | Observable structural cursor controller      |\n| `mode`       | Required | `focus` or `free` binding discriminator      |\n| `pin`        | `false`  | Enables activation to pin and dismiss        |\n\nMode-specific fields are:\n\n| Mode    | Option  | Default       | Meaning                                     |\n| ------- | ------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------- |\n| `focus` | `match` | `xy`          | Semantic axes shared between hosts          |\n| `free`  | `x`     | Scale inverse | Optional x-axis `valueAt` semantic override |\n| `free`  | `y`     | Scale inverse | Optional y-axis `valueAt` semantic override |\n\nA free cursor inverts each coordinate through that destination scene's\nresolved scale by default. Reversed ranges and responsive plot geometry need\nno copied scale. A missing or non-invertible scale requires an explicit\n`valueAt(context)` callback for that axis. Use the callback to replace default\ninversion with observed-value snapping, rounding, or another semantic mapping;\nreturn `undefined` for an intentionally coordinate-only axis.\n\n`createChartCursor` remains a three-method structural store. `cursorHost` opts\nthe binding into platform-neutral cursor policy without adding that policy to\ncharts that do not use cursors. Adapter and renderer authors can import the\nprojection, focus, presentation, and session helpers from\n`@tanstack/charts/cursor/host`; application code normally uses the token from\n`@tanstack/charts/cursor`.\n\n`ChartCursorExtensionToken` is the environment-neutral binding contract.\n`cursorHost` implements it as a `ChartCursorHostExtension`. Adapter authors\ncall `createChartCursorHostSession(binding)` to create an ownership-safe\n`ChartCursorHostSession` for one mounted binding.\n\nThat host entry uses `createFocusChartCursorState` and\n`createFreeChartCursorState` to publish state, then\n`resolveChartCursorPresentation` and `resolveChartCursorFocus` to project it\ninto each scene. `resolveChartPointerFocus` composes built-in axis focus with\npainted containment and returns `undefined` for default nearest focus so a host\ncan apply its spatial index or renderer geometry. `resolveChartFocusStrategy`\nconverts a built-in focus name to its grouping strategy.\n`resolveFocusPresentation` finally produces renderer-neutral underlay and\noverlay nodes. An empty array from `resolveChartPointerFocus` means an explicit\nstrategy found no target. Pass `scene.points` (the default) as its final\nargument to enable painted containment. A distinct array explicitly signals\nactive renderer presentation points and preserves anchor resolution while\nthose interpolated points are authoritative.\n\nUse the same controller in several browser or React Native definitions to\nsynchronize by semantic value rather than pixel position. In `focus` mode,\npointer, responder, keyboard, or accessibility focus publishes a\nvalue-anchored cursor. Each subscribing host maps the value through its own\nscales, resolves its own local point and focus group, and paints its crosshair\nand tooltip. `match` defaults to `xy`; choose `x` or `y` for an axis cursor.\nThe originating point's group is retained as the preferred series when more\nthan one local point matches. Optional `ChartCursorState.origin` carries\n`{ key, markId, datumIndex }` to retain the local point when equal values\nrepeat, including across facets. A unique `key` plus `markId` survives data\nreordering; `datumIndex` disambiguates duplicate keys. A consuming scene\nignores the tie-breaker when its key and mark do not exist, then resolves from\nthe portable semantic `value` and preferred `group`.\n\n`free` mode follows plot coordinates without resolving a datum:\n\n```ts\nconst freeCursor = createChartCursor<Date, number>()\n\nconst definition = defineChart(baseDefinition, {\n  cursor: {\n    use: cursorHost,\n    controller: freeCursor,\n    mode: 'free',\n    pin: true,\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe host publishes normalized plot coordinates so the free cursor survives a\nresponsive relayout, while resolved-scale inversion adds semantic values for\nlabels and application state. `valueAt` is an explicit replacement when the\ninteraction needs snapping or another precision policy. Programmatic state may\ninstead use `anchor: 'value'`,\n`anchor: 'normalized'`, or `anchor: 'scene'`. Only the anchor's coordinate\nspace is authoritative; the other fields are diagnostics from the host that\nlast emitted the state.\n\n`createChartCursor` returns `getState`, `setState`, and `subscribe`. Passing\n`null` clears the cursor, and `setState` accepts a previous-state updater.\nUnpinned host-owned state clears on pointer or responder cancellation, leave,\nand blur. Cleanup is ownership-safe: a host clears only the exact unpinned\nstate object it most recently published to that controller. State replaced by\nthe application or another host, and pinned state, survives that host's\ncleanup, rebinding, and unmount.\n\nWith `pin: true`, click or tap pins. Browser focus cursors toggle through Enter\nor Space and dismiss through Escape; React Native focus cursors expose the\nequivalent activate and escape accessibility actions. A pinned free cursor can\nbe toggled by another click or tap, or cleared programmatically.\n\nFree mode deliberately does not invent keyboard datum navigation. When its\nvalues are part of the reader's task, pair it with a labeled semantic control\nor status output. A focus-mode cursor inherits the chart's existing keyboard\nnavigation and accessible tooltip behavior.\n\nDefinition cursor bindings have browser and React Native host parity. DOM\nrenderer hosts publish pointer and keyboard interaction. React Native `Chart`\npublishes responder gestures and, for focus mode, accessibility navigation,\nactivation, and dismissal. Both hosts subscribe to programmatic updates and\nshared controllers. Free mode deliberately remains a coordinate gesture with\nno invented keyboard or accessibility datum order.\n\n## Controlled keyed selection\n\nImport controlled semantic selection from its exact subpath:\n\n```ts\nimport { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\nimport { keyedSelection, whenSelected } from '@tanstack/charts/selection'\n```\n\n`keyedSelection(options)` returns a `KeyedSelection` controller. Its\n`KeyedSelectionOptions` contain:\n\n| Option     | Meaning                                                                  |\n| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `selected` | Controlled signal holding a semantic key or `null`, with typed reasons   |\n| `key`      | Maps a datum and `{ point }` context to a semantic key or a nullish skip |\n\nThe key callback's second parameter is a typed `KeyedSelectionKeyContext`.\nControlled interaction state uses `ControlledSignal<TValue, TReason>`; its\nchange callback receives the proposed value and a\n`ControlledSignalChangeContext<TReason>` containing `{ reason }`.\n\nPlace that controller on `ChartDefinitionOptions.selection`. Pointer click,\nEnter, and Space pass the activated point to the controller. A blank-surface\nclick clears an existing selection. The controlled signal proposes a complete\nreplacement; Charts does not mutate its snapshot. Rebuild the definition with\nthe accepted value.\n\n`KeyedSelectionChange` is a discriminated union:\n\n- `{ type: 'select', value, point, source }` proposes a non-null semantic key;\n- `{ type: 'clear', value: null, point: null, source }` proposes clearing it.\n\n`source` is the `ChartSelectionSource` value `pointer` or `keyboard`. Selecting\nthe currently selected key still emits `select`, so application policy can\ndecide whether repeated activation is a no-op or another action. Returning\n`null` or `undefined` from `key` ignores activation for that point.\n\n`whenSelected(mark, selection)` returns the same ordinary mark filtered by the\ncontrolled key after domains resolve. The full mark still contributes scale\nchannels and domains. Its final geometry retains every fragment owned by a\nmatching point, while interaction metadata and points are removed. It is\ntherefore a decorative overlay and cannot create a duplicate focus or\nactivation target. Duplicate semantic keys intentionally paint every match.\n\nSelection does not replace focus, tooltip pinning, or application UI. A linked\nHTML table, live status, clear action, and persistence remain outside the\ndefinition and can update the same controlled key.\n\n## Continuous cursor\n\nImport `continuousCursor` from `@tanstack/charts/interaction/cursor` and place\nit in `ChartDefinitionOptions.controls`. Its `position` is a controlled\n`ContinuousCursorPosition<TXValue, TYValue> | null`. Both values must be\nnumbers or dates backed by invertible scales.\n\n`ContinuousCursorChange` reports one of:\n\n- `preview` for pointer or touch movement, leave, and cancellation;\n- `commit` when click or tap proposes a pinned position; or\n- `clear` when a pinned cursor is toggled off or Escape is pressed.\n\nEvery reason includes the proposed `value`, the controlled `origin`, a\n`source`, and a more specific `cause`. Rebuild the definition with an accepted\ncommit or clear. Pointer previews remain host-local while the controlled value\nis `null`, avoiding a chart-scene render for each movement.\n\n`xRule`, `yRule`, and `marker` configure renderer-neutral guide geometry and\nare enabled by default. `xLabel` and `yLabel` are opt-in and accept semantic\nformatters plus side, offset, padding, background, text, stroke, and typography\noptions. The behavior resolves all geometry against the final plot and scale\nranges.\n\nSVG and Canvas DOM hosts mount the same contained pointer overlay. Static SVG\nand React Native retain only the guide for an accepted non-null position. The\noverlay is presentation-only, so applications should pair it with semantic\nsliders or inputs, status text, and any persistence or rounding policy.\n\nThis differs from `focusGuideX` and `focusGuideY`: focus guides retarget to a\ndatum-owned chart point and participate in mark motion, while\n`continuousCursor` tracks an unsnapped value pair without creating a focus or\nselection point.\n\nThe public cursor types divide state, event, and presentation concerns:\n\n| Type                                        | Contract                                                           |\n| ------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `ContinuousCursorValue`                     | A finite `number` or valid `Date`                                  |\n| `ContinuousCursorPosition<TX, TY>`          | The semantic x/y pair                                              |\n| `ContinuousCursorPointerSource`             | Pointer or touch preview/commit source                             |\n| `ContinuousCursorSource`                    | Pointer, touch, or keyboard clear source                           |\n| `ContinuousCursorChange<TX, TY>`            | Preview, commit, and clear reason union                            |\n| `ContinuousCursorRuleOptions`               | Rule stroke, opacity, width, dash, and cap                         |\n| `ContinuousCursorMarkerOptions`             | Marker radius, fill, stroke, and opacity                           |\n| `ContinuousCursorLabelOptions<TValue>`      | Formatter, side, spacing, box, text, stroke, and typography        |\n| `ContinuousCursorOptions<TXValue, TYValue>` | Controlled position plus x/y rule, marker, and label configuration |\n\n## Horizontal brush\n\nImport `brushX` from `@tanstack/charts/interaction/brush` and place it in\n`ChartDefinitionOptions.controls`. Its `range` is a controlled\n`BrushRange<TValue>` with inclusive semantic `start` and `end` values.\n\n`BrushXChange` reports `preview`, `commit`, or `cancel`, the proposed and\norigin ranges, the pointer or keyboard source, and whether the selection,\nstart, end, or a new blank region was manipulated. Explicit `values` determine\norder, snapping, slider indices, and keyboard steps. String values require\nthem. Number and Date values may instead use scale inversion only when\n`keyboard: false`.\n\n`BrushXValuesOptions<TValue>` is the candidate-backed form.\n`format` supplies each keyboard handle's value text. Its\n`BrushXFormatContext` names the `start` or `end` handle, so values that mark\nboundaries between items, such as bar edges, can name the first item after the\nstart handle and the last item before the end handle.\n`BrushXContinuousOptions<number | Date>` is the invertible, pointer-only form.\n`BrushXSource` distinguishes pointer and keyboard commits, while\n`BrushXTarget` identifies the selection, start handle, end handle, or newly\ndrawn region.\n\nThe behavior resolves against the final x scale and plot bounds. SVG and\nCanvas DOM hosts mount the same D3-backed overlay and contain its events before\nnormal chart focus or selection. Static SVG and React Native paint the\nrenderer-neutral range and handles without interactive host controls. The\napplication still owns fixed-window expansion, validation, linked-view layout,\nstatus text, persistence, and any native semantic control.\n\n## Horizontal scale handle\n\nImport `handleX` from `@tanstack/charts/interaction/handle` and place it in\n`ChartDefinitionOptions.controls`. It binds one controlled x value to an\nexplicit ordered candidate list. The controlled value must equal one of those\ncandidates.\n\n`HandleXOptions<TXValue, TYValue>` contains:\n\n| Option        | Contract                                                               |\n| ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `value`       | `ControlledSignal<TXValue, HandleXChange<TXValue>>`                    |\n| `values`      | Ordered candidates for scale positioning, snapping, and keyboard steps |\n| `cross`       | `{ edge: 'top' \\| 'bottom', offset? }` or `{ value: TYValue }`         |\n| `trackStyle`  | Scene style for the horizontal candidate track                         |\n| `ruleStyle`   | Scene style for the vertical rule, or `false`                          |\n| `handleStyle` | Scene style for the current-value handle                               |\n| `hitSize`     | Pointer target height and end padding; defaults to `44`                |\n| `ariaLabel`   | Slider name; defaults to `Horizontal value`                            |\n| `format`      | Formats the semantic value for `aria-valuetext`                        |\n| `keyboard`    | Enables the slider keyboard task; defaults to `true`                   |\n| `id`          | Stable behavior and host-control identity                              |\n\nAn edge cross places the track outside the corresponding plot edge by its\noptional offset. A value cross maps the supplied semantic y value through the\nfinal y scale and places the track inside the plot. For an edge cross, the\nvertical rule spans from the opposite plot edge to the track. For a value\ncross, it spans from the plot top to that value.\n\n`HandleXCross<TValue>` names this edge-or-value union.\n`HandleXPointerSource` is `pointer | touch`; `HandleXSource` adds `keyboard`\nfor committed and canceled changes.\n\n`HandleXChange<TValue>` is a discriminated union. Pointer and touch gestures\npropose `preview`, then `commit` on release or `cancel` with the origin value.\nArrow keys move one candidate, Home and End move to the first and last\ncandidate, and every keyboard change commits immediately. Each reason carries\nthe proposed `value`, gesture `origin`, and pointer, touch, or keyboard\n`source`.\n\nThe behavior resolves against the final x and optional y scales. SVG and\nCanvas DOM hosts mount the same contained slider overlay and preserve an\nactive gesture across accepted controlled updates. Static SVG and React Native\npaint the accepted track, rule, and handle without an interactive control.\nApplications retain playback timing, status, persistence, and native semantic\ncontrols.\n\n## Horizontal zoom\n\nImport `zoomX` from `@tanstack/charts/interaction/zoom` and place it in\n`ChartDefinitionOptions.controls`. Its `window` is a controlled\n`ZoomXWindow<TValue>` with semantic `start` and `end` values. `TValue` must be\na number or Date backed by an invertible x scale.\n\n`extent` defines the complete allowed x domain. `scaleExtent` defaults to\n`[1, Infinity]`; its first value must be `1`, and its second value sets the\nmaximum zoom factor relative to the extent. The configured x scale must use\nthe controlled window as its domain; accepted proposals rebuild the definition\nwith that same window.\n\nThe behavior resolves against the final x scale and plot bounds. Its contained\nSVG or Canvas host control owns pointer-anchored vertical-wheel zoom, drag and\nhorizontal-wheel pan, pointer and touch input, delta-mode normalization,\nclamping, cancellation, and teardown. `wheelActivation` defaults to `focus`, so\nwheel input is captured only while the plot control is focused. Use `modifier`\nto capture Control+wheel or Command+wheel under the pointer without prior focus\nwhile every unmodified wheel continues to scroll the page. Use `always` only\nwhen the chart should capture every wheel over its plot. Plus and minus zoom\naround the center, arrow keys pan, Home proposes the full extent, and Escape\ncancels an active gesture.\n\n`ZoomXChange` reports `preview`, `commit`, or `cancel`; the proposed `value`,\nthe gesture `origin`, a `zoom`, `pan`, or `reset` action, and a wheel, pointer,\ntouch, or keyboard source. Wheel streams and direct manipulation preview before\ntheir terminal event. Keyboard changes commit immediately. A cancel proposes\nthe origin window.\n\nSet `keyboard: false` only when an application supplies equivalent semantic\ncontrols. `ariaLabel` names the plot control, `ariaDescription` can replace its\ngenerated instructions, `format` formats values in those instructions, and\n`onActiveChange` observes focus without moving accepted state into Charts.\n\nCharts does not mutate the controlled snapshot. Keep visible-row filtering or\nclipping, y-domain policy, status, reset and recovery controls, follow-latest\nbehavior, and persistence in the application. Static SVG and React Native\nrender the accepted configured window but provide no zoom host control.\n\nThe public zoom types are:\n\n| Type                   | Contract                                                                 |\n| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `ZoomXValue`           | A finite `number` or valid `Date`                                        |\n| `ZoomXWindow<TValue>`  | Semantic `start` and `end` values                                        |\n| `ZoomXSource`          | Wheel, pointer, touch, or keyboard origin                                |\n| `ZoomXAction`          | Zoom, pan, or reset action                                               |\n| `ZoomXWheelActivation` | Focus, modifier-gated, or unconditional wheel capture                    |\n| `ZoomXChange<TValue>`  | Preview, commit, and cancel reason union                                 |\n| `ZoomXOptions<TValue>` | Controlled window, extent, limits, wheel, accessibility, and key options |\n\n## Disabling chart-owned focus\n\nSet `focus: false` to disable chart-owned pointer and keyboard point focus. The\nscene keeps its semantic points but omits the generated default focus layer,\nand the DOM host forces the chart surface out of the tab order. Explicit\nfocus-only marks remain available to custom renderers and programmatic paint.\n\n```ts\nimport { focusDisabled } from '@tanstack/charts/focus/disabled'\n```\n\n`focusDisabled` resolves, groups, and navigates to no points. Use it when an\napplication owns gestures, selection paint, accessibility, and task semantics\noutside the native resolver but still needs the rendered focus layer. Set\ndefinition `keyboard: false` and omit its `tooltip` as appropriate for that\napplication-owned interaction.\n\n## Custom focus strategies\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartFocusStrategy<\n  TDatum = unknown,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n> {\n  resolve(\n    points: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[],\n    context: ChartFocusResolveContext,\n  ): readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[]\n\n  group(\n    points: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[],\n    context: ChartFocusGroupContext<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>,\n  ): readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[]\n\n  navigation(\n    points: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[],\n  ): readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[]\n}\n```\n\n`ChartFocusResolveContext` contains scene-pixel `x`, `y`, and `maxDistance`.\n`resolve` returns the primary point first. `ChartFocusGroupContext` contains\nthe point restored or reached through keyboard navigation. `navigation`\nreturns the ordered keyboard task set.\n\n`ChartFocusMode` accepts a `ChartFocusPreset` string or a\n`ChartFocusStrategy`.\n\n## Tooltips\n\nImport `tooltip` from `@tanstack/charts/tooltip` and place it on the definition\nfor the native accessible tooltip. The default is a structured label-value\ntable. Grouped focus adds a shared-axis heading and one color swatch and value\nrow per series. Visible axis labels are reused. Numbers use browser locale\nformatting; dates use stable UTC ISO formatting.\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartTooltipOptions<\n  TDatum = unknown,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n> {\n  className?: string\n  portal?: ChartTooltipPortalInput\n  items?: readonly ChartTooltipItem<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[]\n  sort?: ChartTooltipSort<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>\n  anchor?: ChartTooltipAnchor<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>\n  placement?: 'auto' | ChartTooltipPlacement | readonly ChartTooltipPlacement[]\n  offset?: number\n  content?: (\n    points: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[],\n    context: ChartTooltipContentContext,\n  ) => ChartTooltipContent\n  format?: (\n    point: ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>,\n    context: ChartTooltipContentContext,\n  ) => string\n  formatGroup?: (\n    points: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[],\n    context: ChartTooltipContentContext,\n  ) => string\n  sticky?: boolean\n  visibility?: 'focus' | 'pinned'\n}\n```\n\n| Option        | Default        | Meaning                                               |\n| ------------- | -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n| `className`   | None           | Class appended after `ts-chart-tooltip`               |\n| `portal`      | None           | Optional top-layer or fixed-position transport        |\n| `items`       | Automatic x/y  | Ordered rows for a single focused point               |\n| `sort`        | `visual`       | Grouped row order                                     |\n| `anchor`      | `point`        | Preset, per-axis coordinates, or coordinate resolver  |\n| `placement`   | `auto`         | Fixed or ordered fallback box placements              |\n| `offset`      | `10`           | Scene-pixel gap between anchor and box                |\n| `content`     | Automatic rows | Returns a safe title and structured rows              |\n| `format`      | None           | Replaces content with primary-point text              |\n| `formatGroup` | None           | Replaces content with focused-group text              |\n| `sticky`      | `true`         | Enables activation-to-pin and text selection          |\n| `visibility`  | `focus`        | Shows on focus or only after activation with `pinned` |\n\nFormatting precedence is `content`, `formatGroup`, `format`, then the default.\nThe text formatters do not parse HTML, and newlines are preserved. `className`\nis appended to `ts-chart-tooltip`.\n\nThe default DOM surface inherits these optional CSS variables from the chart\ncontainer while retaining its built-in fallback values. A portaled Popover\nkeeps that ancestry. If the portal fallback moves the tooltip under\n`ownerDocument.body`, chart-container-only variables no longer inherit; set\nthem on the tooltip class or a shared document ancestor instead:\n\n| Variable                           | Controls                    |\n| ---------------------------------- | --------------------------- |\n| `--ts-chart-tooltip-background`    | Surface background          |\n| `--ts-chart-tooltip-color`         | Text color                  |\n| `--ts-chart-tooltip-border`        | Complete border declaration |\n| `--ts-chart-tooltip-border-radius` | Corner radius               |\n| `--ts-chart-tooltip-shadow`        | Box shadow                  |\n| `--ts-chart-tooltip-max-width`     | Maximum width               |\n| `--ts-chart-tooltip-padding`       | Inner spacing               |\n| `--ts-chart-tooltip-font`          | Complete font shorthand     |\n\nUse `className` for content structure or additional selectors. Use the\nvariables for ordinary surface theming without specificity overrides.\n\n`ChartTooltipContentContext.pinned` is `false` during transient inspection and\n`true` after activation. `content`, `format`, `formatGroup`, and item `text`\nreceive the same context, so either structured or plaintext content can reveal\nadditional detail when pinned.\n\n### Ordered point items\n\n`items` is an ordered single-point row list. Use `x`, `y`, and `group`\nshorthands, a configured channel, a scalar datum field, or derived text:\n\n```ts\nconst detailedDefinition = defineChart(definition, {\n  tooltip: {\n    use: tooltip,\n    items: [\n      {\n        channel: 'y',\n        label: 'Revenue',\n        text: (point) => currency(point.yValue),\n      },\n      {\n        field: 'volume',\n        label: 'Volume',\n        text: (point) => compact(point.datum.volume),\n      },\n      {\n        id: 'change',\n        label: 'Change',\n        text: (point) =>\n          point.datum.change == null ? null : percent(point.datum.change),\n      },\n      'x',\n      'group',\n    ],\n  },\n})\n```\n\nArray order is row order. A nullish datum field or nullish `text` result omits\nthe row. Adding `group` to `items` renders it as a row instead of the automatic\nsingle-point title. In grouped focus, the shared-axis item supplies the heading\nlabel and text, the opposite-axis item formats values, and the group item\nformats series names. `sort` orders those generated series rows. Additional\ngrouped structure belongs in `content`.\n\n`sort` accepts `visual`, `color-domain`, `focus`, or a typed point comparator.\nVisual order follows the marks across the screen: top-to-bottom for an x-group\nand left-to-right for a y-group.\n\n### Anchor and placement\n\n`anchor` controls the scene coordinate followed by the box:\n\n- `point` follows the primary focused point.\n- `pointer` follows the current pointer and falls back to the point for\n  keyboard focus.\n- `group-center` uses the center of the focused points' bounding box.\n- `{ x, y }` chooses each coordinate from point, pointer, semantic value,\n  group center, or a plot edge.\n- A resolver receives the focused points plus `{ focus, pointer, plot,\nsurface, scales }` and returns scene coordinates. A nullish or non-finite\n  result falls back to the primary point.\n\n`placement` accepts `auto`, one placement, or an ordered fallback list. The\nplacements are `top`, `top-right`, `right`, `bottom-right`, `bottom`,\n`bottom-left`, `left`, and `top-left`. A single placement is fixed and shifted\ninside the surface. A list uses the first placement that fits; if none fits,\nit uses the least-overflowing candidate and shifts it inside. `auto` uses\n`top`, `bottom`, `right`, then `left`.\n\n```ts\nconst groupedDefinition = defineChart(definition, {\n  tooltip: {\n    use: tooltip,\n    anchor: { x: 'plot-center', y: 'plot-top' },\n    placement: ['top', 'right', 'left', 'bottom'],\n    offset: 12,\n  },\n})\n```\n\nImport `portal` from `@tanstack/charts/tooltip/portal` and assign it to the\ntooltip's `portal` option when an ancestor clips overflow or creates an\nincompatible stacking context. The host opens the tooltip as a manual Popover\nin the browser top layer where supported, while retaining its chart DOM\nancestry. If Popover is unavailable or fails, it moves the tooltip directly\nunder the chart's `ownerDocument` body with fixed high-stack positioning. Both\npaths map the scene anchor to viewport coordinates, reposition during scroll,\nresize, and content resize, and collide against the viewport instead of the\nchart box.\n\nClicking, Enter, or Space pins the tooltip. The next activation unpins it.\nPressing the pointer outside the chart and tooltip, or pressing `Escape`,\nunpins and clears focus. Set `sticky: false` to disable pinning. A display-only\ntooltip has `role=\"status\"` and `aria-live=\"polite\"`.\n\nSet `visibility: 'pinned'` for click-or-keyboard detail that should not paint a\ntransient shell. Focus and inline mark states still update before activation;\nthe tooltip element and adapter body mount only while pinned.\n\n`content` supports display-only rows. Every framework adapter can compose\nnative content around those rows while preserving the definition's ordering,\nanchor, placement, portal, and pinning behavior. A pinned custom body has\nnon-modal dialog semantics.\n\n## Callbacks\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartInteractionCallbacks {\n  onFocusChange?: (point: ChartPoint | null) => void\n  onFocusGroupChange?: (points: readonly ChartPoint[]) => void\n  onSelect?: (point: ChartPoint | null) => void\n}\n```\n\nDefinitions infer callback datum and semantic x/y value types without adapter\ngenerics. Focus callbacks run only when the primary focus key changes, except\nthat a scene rebuild with an existing focused key reports the point with its\nnew coordinates and datum.\n\n`onSelect` reports mouse clicks and keyboard activation. A click with no point\nreports `null`; Enter and Space do nothing until a point is focused. When a\ndefinition has a selection controller, it receives the activation before the\nlegacy callback.\n\n## Spatial indexes\n\nThe default lookup scans the cached scene targets linearly. Dense charts can\ninject an index:\n\n```ts\ntype ChartSpatialIndexFactory<\n  TDatum = unknown,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n> = (\n  points: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[],\n  context: ChartSpatialIndexFactoryContext<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>,\n) => ChartSpatialIndex<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>\n```\n\nThe host rebuilds the index when the scene or definition changes. The index\nowns its search algorithm and must apply `maxDistance`. Point-only factories\ncan ignore the second argument; geometry-aware indexes can traverse\n`context.scene` and use primitive bounds as their acceleration layer.\nUse the granular spatial primitive appropriate to the data; the boundary is\ndescribed in [Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md).\n\nSupplying an index also replaces default primitive containment and affinity\nranking; the host does not add a linear safety scan after an indexed query. An\nindex that wants identical geometry semantics should index scene-primitive\nbounds and perform exact shape checks on its candidates.\n\nA custom `focus` strategy takes precedence over `spatialIndex` for pointer\nresolution.\n\n## Application-owned gestures\n\nDragging, scrolling, custom crosshair overlays, and freeform range or lasso\nselections can listen on a wrapper or use `onRender` to attach application\nbehavior to the default SVG or complete surface. Use `handleX` for one ordered\nscale value, `brushX` for a normal horizontal semantic range, and `zoomX` for a\nnormal controlled x window. For custom gestures, keep semantic state outside\nthe scene and update a responsive definition by replacing its identity.\n\nUse a definition cursor binding for snapped or free crosshairs. Keep other\nsemantic state outside the scene.\n\nFor application-timed datum inspection, use the stable controller instead of\nreimplementing coordinate conversion and focus:\n\n```ts\nconst position = interaction.clientToScene(event.clientX, event.clientY)\nconst target = interaction.resolvePointer(event.clientX, event.clientY)\ninteraction.setControlledFocus(target)\ninteraction.setControlledFocus(null)\n```\n\nThe resolution contains the scene position, primary point, and focus group.\nPassing that resolution to `setControlledFocus` infers `source: 'pointer'` and\nre-resolves its held position after scene and presentation updates. Passing a\nraw `ChartPoint` defaults to `source: 'programmatic'`; either source can be\noverridden explicitly. Controlled focus is not cleared by unrelated\npointer-leave or focus-out events. Pass `{ pinned: true }` to make an enabled\nsticky tooltip interactive.\n\nClean up application listeners before the next attachment or unmount.\nFor a completely independent renderer or interaction layer, use the scene and\nextension contracts in [Custom extensions](./custom-extensions.md).\n"}
